 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120349 1074 "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nSince $A∼B,$ the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the equation $Bx=0,$ which corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=0,$ where ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118202 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318914 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120353 1071 "Find the curvature of the curve $y = 2\\ln (x - 1)$ at $x = 2$." 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120353 1071 "Find the curvature of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ at $x=2.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120353 1074 "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{x-1},\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}}|}{{[1+{(\\frac{2}{x-1})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}{[1+\\frac{4}{(x-1)^{2}}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(2) & =\\frac{2}{(2-1)^{2}{[1+\\frac{4}{(2-1)^{2}}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{5}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118214 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318437 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120355 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120355 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nIf $x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3} = b$, then what is $x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3}$?" 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120355 1074 "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{2} \\\\ 6{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ 9{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=-6 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 9 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 9 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 9 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ {x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ 0=13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0=13$  gives a false statement. So the system has no solution." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118220 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318530 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120356 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120356 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n$c_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}, c_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, d = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nIf $x_{1}c_{1} + x_{2}c_{2} = d$, then what is $x_{1} + x_{2}$?" 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120356 1074 "If ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1} \\\\ 5{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13{x}_{2} & =13\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${x}_{2}=1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4(1) & =3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=1$ into the third equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & =5 \\\\ 5(1)+2(1) & =5 \\\\ 7 & =5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a false statement. \n\nSo, the system has no solution." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118232 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318807 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120358 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$, where\n$b_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, b_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf $[x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $x_{1} + x_{2}$?" 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120358 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120358 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(2)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=(-2)+2=0.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118244 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318458 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120360 1071 "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(-1, - π)$ where $t = π$, given that $‖r^{′}(π)‖ = π$, and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n$T(t) = ⟨\\cos t,-\\sin t⟩$." 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120360 1071 "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(-1,-\\pi )$ where $t=\\pi $, given that $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖=\\pi ,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120360 1074 "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(\\pi )=⟨-1,-\\pi ⟩$,  since the point $P(-1,-\\pi )$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{\\pi }{1}=\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=\\pi $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\pi ) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\pi )}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=⟨0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi )+R(\\pi )N(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,-\\pi ⟩+\\pi ⟨0,1⟩=⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118250 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318470 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120361 1071 "The vectors $a_{1}$, $a_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b ∈ Span{a_{1}, a_{2}}$. In the affirmative case, find the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$ from the decomposition $b = x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2}$.\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120361 1071 "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120361 1074 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=12,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $b$ does *not* lie in $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118268 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318905 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120364 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$, where\n$b_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, b_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}, b_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf $[x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3}$?" 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120364 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120364 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\ 0=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the fourth equation is always false. Therefore, the system has no solution and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx\\notin \\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118274 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318874 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120365 1071 "Let $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}}$ be a basis of $R^{2}$ and $[x]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$. What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$, where $b_{1} =-c_{2}$ and $b_{2} = 3c_{1}$?" 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120365 1071 "Let $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where ${b}_{1}=-{c}_{2}$ and ${b}_{2}=3{c}_{1}?$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120365 1074 "Since $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{c}_{2}+{c}_{1} \\\\  & =2(-{c}_{2})+\\frac{1}{3}(3{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =2{b}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118280 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318799 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120366 1071 "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ be a basis of $R^{3}$ and $[x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$. What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}, c_{3}}$, where $c_{1} =-2b_{2}$, $c_{2} = b_{3}$ and $c_{3} =-3b_{1}$?" 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120366 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=-2{b}_{2}$, ${c}_{2}={b}_{3}$ and ${c}_{3}=-3{b}_{1}?$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120366 1074 "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}+2{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-5{b}_{2}+2{b}_{3}+4{b}_{1} \\\\  & =5(-{b}_{2})+2{b}_{3}-4(-{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(-2{b}_{2})+2{c}_{2}-\\frac{4}{3}(-3{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}-\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118298 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318873 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120369 1071 "If $A$ is a $6 \\cdot 11$ matrix that has $8$ **non-pivot** columns, then $rank(A) =$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120369 1071 "If $A$ is a $6\\times 11$ matrix that has $8$ **non-pivot** columns, then $\\text{rank}(A)=$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120369 1074 "We know that $\\text{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A.$ \n\nSince $A$ has $11$ columns and $8$ of them are non-pivots, then the number of pivot columns in $A$ is \n\n$$\n11-8=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118322 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318593 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120373 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120373 1071 "What is $dim(Span{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}})$?" 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120373 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=1.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118334 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318940 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120375 1071 "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$. Find $Null(A)$." 13194139533419 17592186045585 false] [17592186120375 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186120375 1074 "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is ${x}_{1}$, and we have two free variables, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ in terms of ${x}_{2}$  and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nFrom the first equation we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3},$ so the set of all the solutions of $Ax=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186124496 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319205 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [1759