={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=1$ and $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(1+j\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{2}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(1+\\frac{2j}{n})-3)] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{2}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1+\\frac{8j}{n}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318967 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318766 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318967 1069 "q-345052" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318967 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318967 1071 "![](images/f3/f321b063e3bc76eeb5b426d00cbf94451a06cec928008eaadc965547691c412a.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=4+x$ between $x=0,$ $x=4$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a right Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $i.$*\n\n${S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318967 1072 #uuid "1af811e2-d217-57d3-a9b2-d6a86ea6f17b" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318967 1074 "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=4$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=4,$ so the step size is \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-0}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}f(0+i\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}f(i) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}(4+i).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318546 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318968 1069 "q-345063" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318968 1071 "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x)=5\\sqrt{x}-2$ on the interval $[4,9].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318968 1072 #uuid "a9eb0f85-ea20-5408-b6ef-a6a801e3cb2e" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318968 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[4,9]$ and differentiable on $(4,9),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (4,9)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=4$ and $b=9$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(9)-f(4)}{9-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(5\\sqrt{9}-2)-(5\\sqrt{4}-2)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-8}{5} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\sqrt{x}-2)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{c}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{c}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{c} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ c & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318564 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318969 1069 "q-345076" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318969 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318969 1071 "Find $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)-4y(t)=-4,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)-s-4Y(s)=-\\frac{4}{s}.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318969 1072 #uuid "77cd7be7-8bc7-5670-aea2-bdee8c6eb4f3" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318969 1074 "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)-s-4Y(s) & =-\\frac{4}{s} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-4)Y(s) & =s-\\frac{4}{s} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-4)Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-4}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-4}{s({s}^{2}-4)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is valid for $s>2.$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}(Y(s)) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\ y(t) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318514 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318970 1069 "q-345078" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318970 1071 "Find $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-9y=-8{e}^{-t},\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)-2s-2-9Y(s)=-\\frac{8}{s+1}.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318970 1072 #uuid "403b58d0-4bf5-5f05-968b-3eb6100a4249" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318970 1074 "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)-2s-2-9Y(s) & =-\\frac{8}{s+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-9)Y(s) & =2s+2-\\frac{8}{s+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-9)Y(s) & =\\frac{2({s}^{2}+2s-3)}{s+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-9)Y(s) & =\\frac{2(s+3)(s-1)}{s+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{2(s+3)(s-1)}{(s+1)(s-3)(s+3)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{2(s-1)}{(s+1)(s-3)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{s-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is valid for $s>3.$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}(Y(s)) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{s-3}} \\\\ y(t) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+1}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-3}} \\\\ y(t) & ={e}^{-t}+{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318971 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318910 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318971 1069 "q-345083" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318971 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318971 1071 "Find $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)+y(t)=7,\\,y(0)=7,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)+Y(s)=\\frac{7(1+{s}^{2})}{s}.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318971 1072 #uuid "f4ec3a28-c964-5478-9896-c04da23c6e67" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318971 1074 "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+Y(s) & =\\frac{7(1+{s}^{2})}{s} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{7(1+{s}^{2})}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{7}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is valid for $s>0.$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}(Y(s)) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{7}{s}} \\\\ y(t) & =7{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\ y(t) & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318842 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318972 1069 "q-345084" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318972 1071 "Find $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)+y(t)=2{t}^{4}+24{t}^{2},\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)+Y(s)=\\frac{48(1+{s}^{2})}{{s}^{5}}.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318972 1072 #uuid "b65b9d2f-a250-5dfc-8f19-d71c41aa6eb2" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318972 1074 "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+Y(s) & =\\frac{48(1+{s}^{2})}{{s}^{5}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{48(1+{s}^{2})}{{s}^{5}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{48}{{s}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is valid for $s>0.$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}(Y(s)) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{48}{{s}^{5}}} \\\\ y(t) & =2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{24}{{s}^{5}}} \\\\ y(t) & =2{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318660 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318973 1069 "q-345088" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318973 1071 "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-2{e}^{-4t}+5{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{17}.\n$$\n\nFind, in terms of $t$ only, an expression for the **current** $I$ in this circuit, in amperes.\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318973 1072 #uuid "a15ce12f-67af-595a-8feb-031dd1000329" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318973 1074 "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-2{e}^{-4t}+5{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{17}) \\\\  & =-5{e}^{-t}+8{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318508 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318974 1069 "q-345094" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318974 1071 "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)={e}^{-2t}-3{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318974 1072 #uuid "e95a4d25-6c9d-57b1-8d08-3197c0c476a5" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318974 1074 "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{-2t}-3{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-2t}+15{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}+15{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{8}{5})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{36+64}{25}}=2.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318580 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318975 1069 "q-345095" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318975 1071 "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-2{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318975 1072 #uuid "e99f439d-ed1a-5653-bd3b-a57694b6cd74" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318975 1074 "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-2{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=2{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{10}{13})}^{2}+{(\\frac{24}{13})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{676}{169}}=2.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318584 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318976 1069 "q-345096" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318976 1071 "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=4{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318976 1072 #uuid "6d2c69af-dd17-5c0b-85fa-4c79b0a97b43" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318976 1074 "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =-8{e}^{-2t}+6{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-8{e}^{-2t}+6{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{60}{17})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{25}{17})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{4225}{289}}=\\frac{65}{17}.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318857 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318977 1069 "q-345103" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318977 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $24\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318977 1072 #uuid "a5536751-fcf7-5c91-932b-4c365b86809a" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318977 1074 "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/64/6494f1905ab90cb3d5655aaa824726255dc7332841ec656dc88c2180b0f93a1c.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318610 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318978 1069 "q-345105" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318978 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $15\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318978 1072 #uuid "4d7efbe4-b551-57a3-9a49-0e74c5bd54a4" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318978 1074 "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/a0/a051a6380df51ba3c1141bc69952ba442dc9c816df30c5640846894fe1ba4c31.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=15\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=15I=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318697 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318979 1069 "q-345112" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318979 1071 "![](images/a7/a73d5f0c60062001e947301760fe1cdae7e0e17c7fcc9870baf9d0262070b238.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318979 1072 #uuid "c75c18d9-d88e-5fd0-b738-dee782dbc767" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318979 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.025\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.025}=40q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+40q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318911 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318980 1069 "q-345115" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318980 1071 "![](images/8c/8cc2afe50a1f1e3ed0a173cdba6f5a5f59145b0afa6038bee7c9a1fe720238a6.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318980 1072 #uuid "50bfaf9e-5fa3-5ce2-93c0-cd914192855f" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318980 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.125\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.125}=8q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318696 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318981 1069 "q-345120" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318981 1071 "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=4{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{5}{11}.\n$$\n\nFind, in terms of $t$ only, an expression for the **current** $I$ in this circuit, in amperes.\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318981 1072 #uuid "9538c7cd-06c1-5575-86de-72f831f368ef" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318981 1074 "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{5}{11}) \\\\  & =-12{e}^{-3t}+12{e}^{-6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318509 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318982 1069 "q-345122" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318982 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=24.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318982 1072 #uuid "c7bd95ee-cca5-57b9-9825-7daba3c299ee" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318982 1074 "The *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0,\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the steady-state charge, we substitute these values into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=24\n$$\n\nSubstituting the steady-state conditions gives\n\n$$\n0+4(0)+8q=24,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n8q=24\\,⟹\\,q=3.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current $I=\\frac{dq}{dt}$ has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $3$ coulombs." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318444 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318983 1069 "q-345127" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318983 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=6\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318983 1072 #uuid "8d91e5be-fcc2-5907-a8d6-f32f94f6bce2" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318983 1074 "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/d7/d7145f4b394e7675b87ed851e344756ecb8441ce737f8664fbfd4b0e426ef36f.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/10)}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=6\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=6\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=6\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n6\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=15.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n6(0)+30(0)+10q=15,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n10q=15\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{15}{10}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{2}$ coulombs." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318658 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318984 1069 "q-34513" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318984 1071 "Given the matrix \n$G=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(G).$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318984 1072 #uuid "bdf77374-96bf-579c-a597-3f47425959ec" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318984 1074 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(G),$ we reduce the matrix $G$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 2 & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}^{′} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, only the $1$st column is a pivot column, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st column from the original matrix is ${g}_{1}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(G)$ is ${{g}_{1}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318653 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318985 1069 "q-345130" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318985 1071 "![](images/50/50bb98a563a0ee061a0faa4d4390aaf459a2fdfccb4721d5d7e6ddd3102c98b1.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318985 1072 #uuid "1e4d4641-9b55-5517-bac3-9fc3e587385e" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318985 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I=12\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+12\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=6.\n$$\n\nThe **steady-state charge** is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response (the part of the solution that depends on the initial conditions) eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n4(0)+12(0)+4q=6,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n4q=6\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{6}{4}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, the inductor acts as a short circuit, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{2}$ coulombs." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318446 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318986 1069 "q-345132" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318986 1071 "![](images/7c/7cbcdf7d43a99b9558b1d085d2306c22e1d27134a5b0ea428721ce7bd074b725.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{P}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{Q}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318986 1072 #uuid "fbfa339e-c615-578f-b729-cc9c3ed650e1" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318986 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $5\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=5\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=5I=5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q+\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+5(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ +4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (3\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-5\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ -5\\alpha +3\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{5}{17},$ $\\beta =\\frac{3}{17}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-5,$ $Q=3,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-5+3=-2.\n$$" 1319413953341