w, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+2{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+3(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\, &  \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ \\alpha +3\\beta =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{7}{10},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{7}{10})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}}≈0.71,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318988 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318923 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318988 1069 "q-34514" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318988 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318988 1071 "Given the matrix \n$F=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ Find a