\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+\\frac{8t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318937 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318995 1069 "q-345208" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318995 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318995 1071 "A body of mass $9\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{2t})$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318995 1072 #uuid "bbcdde8a-39f6-5e49-8b19-cc84bb2be24b" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318995 1074 "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{2t})] \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2t})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2t})\\cdot 2{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =-6{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in N