 meters per second. \n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318998 1072 #uuid "d70a9d0d-47bd-5a88-b909-b1c15e29fe59" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318998 1074 "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{\\frac{t}{2}}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\frac{t}{2}}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\frac{t}{2}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\frac{t}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =2{e}^{\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =2(3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =ma(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)|=|1|=1\\,\\text{N}.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318547 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318999 1069 "q-34523" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318999 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318999 1071 "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. Given that $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318999 1072 #uuid "d82ffeff-7804-5a76-874b-dd255792a433" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186318999 1074 "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nSince $A∼B,$ the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the equation $Bx=0,$ which corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the only solution of $Ax=0$ is $x=0.$ So, $\\text{Null}(A)={0},$ which means that the basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ is the empty set, i.e., $B={\\,}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318875 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319000 1069 "q-345272" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319000 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319000 1071 "$$\n4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+(x+3)y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319000 1072 #uuid "8fb5ceb7-398f-5abf-bba8-56408ac9272d" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319000 1074 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4{x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -5x{y}^{′} & =-5x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (x+3)y & =(x+3){\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(x+3)y={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}+3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -5x{y}^{′} & =-5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5\\lambda +3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319001 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318892 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319001 1069 "q-345275" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319001 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319001 1071 "$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-11x{y}^{′}-(x-4)y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a