{\\lambda +n+1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-(x-4)y=-{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n-(x-4)y=-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}+4{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =2({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -11x{y}^{′} & =-11\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-11(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-11\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+4{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (2{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -11\\lambda +4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318600 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319002 1069 "q-345279" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319002 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319002 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+12x{y}^{′}+(9x-2)y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 12x{y}^{′} & =12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (9x-2)y & =-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(9{a}_{n-1}-2{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319002 1072 #uuid "2f15206c-389e-5969-948f-ad93b00e844b" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319002 1074 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 12x{y}^{′} & =12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (9x-2)y & =-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(9{a}_{n-1}-2{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (9{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +12\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda -1)(3\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-2/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318841 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319003 1069 "q-345281" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319003 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319003 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n8{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+6x{y}^{′}+(8x-3)y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 6x{y}^{′} & =6\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (8x-3)y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(8{a}_{n-1}-3{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319003 1072 #uuid "53354b57-f813-51e1-a379-13c900073c76" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319003 1074 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 6x{y}^{′} & =6\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\ (8x-3)y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(8{a}_{n-1}-3{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +6\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda -3)(2\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/2}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/4}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319004 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318642 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319004 1069 "q-345282" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319004 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319004 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+(6x+1)y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (6x+1)y & ={a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(6{a}_{n-1}+{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319004 1072 #uuid "064a82f0-c307-5a5d-bd8a-b24be351e650" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319004 1074 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\ (6x+1)y & ={a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(6{a}_{n-1}+{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda -1)(2\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318703 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319005 1069 "q-345310" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319005 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319005 1071 "Find  the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x)=2{log}_{2}⁡x$ on the interval $[1,4].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319005 1072 #uuid "923e961f-59d7-5d6a-a213-3c7b9d0f3ac0" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319005 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[1,4]$ and differentiable on $(1,4),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (1,4)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=1$ and $b=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡4-2{log}_{2}⁡1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-0}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{log}_{2}⁡x)=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{2}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ c & =\\frac{3}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318739 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319006 1069 "q-345312" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319006 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319006 1071 "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x)={x}^{4}-x$ on the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319006 1072 #uuid "cb5e2356-85b0-5a3a-a342-aa181560b1ac" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319006 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem.  \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (-1,1)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=-1$ and $b=1$ into the above gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(1)-f(-1)}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{({1}^{4}-1)-((-1)^{4}-(-1))}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}-x)=4{x}^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=4{c}^{3}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{c}^{3}-1 & =-1 \\\\ c & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318559 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319007 1069 "q-345316" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319007 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319007 1071 "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x)={x}^{2}+1$ on the interval $[-1,3].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319007 1072 #uuid "98179bb9-ed40-5749-b68a-ff512cac0e5e" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319007 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem.  \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (-1,3)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=-1$ and $b=3$ into the above gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-1)}{3-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{({3}^{2}+1)-((-1)^{2}+1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-2}{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1)=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=2c.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =2 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318497 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319008 1069 "q-345319" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319008 1071 "![](images/0a/0a4b4be4f7de918b847245372077e159f60f3834bb37ef00f4873e63b4cea894.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y={e}^{x}$ and the $x$-axis between $x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)$ and $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319008 1072 #uuid "14e2c606-5b8f-55fd-b7f1-94cc0c121c9a" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319008 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}{|}_{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318592 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319009 1069 "q-345320" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319009 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319009 1071 "![](images/92/927b979152097aef3939fa78738dbd2deb9d297cadf2d9894c19f31ca76aee1b.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+1$ and the $x$-axis between $x=0$ and $x=\\pi $, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319009 1072 #uuid "d5eb165b-6f5a-57c7-8247-2615e892795c" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319009 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+x){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\pi )-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+0) \\\\  & =(1+\\pi )-(-1) \\\\  & =\\pi +2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318860 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319010 1069 "q-345321" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319010 1071 "![](images/21/2102626f331aaa7803462f8e427ef2c0150deb554955199f3d402123934e815a.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+3$ and the $x$-axis between $x=-1$ and $x=1$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319010 1072 #uuid "a4258571-e2ee-5711-b11c-465f2f2d9827" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319010 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+3x){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{2}{3}+3)-(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{3}-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{12}-(-\\frac{25}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318776 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319011 1069 "q-345322" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319011 1071 "![](images/66/66bf8c1144de1ad65938345d2937fddd6fb43ec4822a2b98a9b5383ca2609567.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-2{x}^{2}+3$ and the $x$-axis between $x=0$ and $x=1$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319011 1072 #uuid "f6b35b94-1570-5f29-9f5d-68c5388281d4" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319011 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+3x){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{2}{3}+3)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319012 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318837 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319012 1069 "q-345338" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319012 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319012 1071 "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319012 1072 #uuid "631ab220-78bb-571b-a8c8-ae85d27d38d6" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319012 1074 "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319013 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318749 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319013 1069 "q-345339" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319013 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319013 1071 "A body of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319013 1072 #uuid "e8f894f8-195c-59e0-8b11-05cf91764fb4" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319013 1074 "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318575 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319014 1069 "q-345340" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319014 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319014 1071 "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319014 1072 #uuid "4a5942b5-b345-55e9-98fa-6a0c078bad31" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319014 1074 "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-1}] \\\\  & =-{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318810 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319015 1069 "q-345343" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319015 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319015 1071 "![](images/34/34eb73b6bd93f9a45b666ed0599719a09b54948d1d81b14ceea9b46f99b75054.png)\n\nFind the area between the curve $y=-{x}^{2}-1$, the $x$-axis, and the line $x=-1$ and $x=1$ (shown above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319015 1072 #uuid "9acdaefd-0b19-529b-95bd-f2d420ca911c" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319015 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{-1}^{1}-x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3})-(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318513 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319016 1069 "q-345345" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319016 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319016 1071 "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t>0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={e}^{2t}$ meters.\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319016 1072 #uuid "bc207cc0-1ae7-5ed9-9b6c-a4cf1fe5e4d1" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319016 1074 "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =4{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}) & =4{e}^{2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =4{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}]} \\\\  & =4{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}) & =ma(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(2)|=|2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318864 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319017 1069 "q-345381" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319017 1071 "![](images/dc/dce89dbea62566c0ee897bffe0638458970799bafdc7d7c8e5084328fef38bef.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=-1$ and $x=1$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319017 1072 #uuid "af180c34-7e0c-5b7b-9e5b-5a6251599f15" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319017 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-arcsin\\,(\\frac{x}{2}){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-(arcsin\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-arcsin\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318540 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319018 1069 "q-345382" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319018 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319018 1071 "A body of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-8t$ meters per second.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}}  $\\text{s}$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319018 1072 #uuid "0f7a6b52-3478-5ae0-b5cf-e85d41d024b9" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319018 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-8t) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =4 \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319019 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318492 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319019 1069 "q-345385" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319019 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319019 1071 "![](images/d4/d43d7419f631b479478641ac203c6816693c68ffc40ccc1d4a8a72da0a03461a.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319019 1072 #uuid "ac6cc325-8109-5df6-a8fa-f84e9e1ea235" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319019 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =arccos\\,(\\frac{x}{3}){|}_{0}^{3/2} \\\\  & =arccos\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-arccos\\,(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318768 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319020 1069 "q-345388" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319020 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319020 1071 "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=(2t-9){e}^{-2t}$ meters per second.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}}  $\\text{s}$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319020 1072 #uuid "4ca153e4-be3b-5464-8d35-cfd52b62cce4" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319020 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2t-9){e}^{-2t}] \\\\  & =(2t-9)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-2t})+{e}^{-2t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-9) \\\\  & =(2t-9)(-2{e}^{-2t})+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =(-4t+18){e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =(-4t+20){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4t+20){e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ -4t+20 & =0 \\\\ -4t & =-20 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=5\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318730 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319021 1069 "q-345390" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319021 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319021 1071 "A body of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=4{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+12t+24$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319021 1072 #uuid "1f53bff2-b6cd-53cb-af8f-3a936c1a2ec6" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319021 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(4{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+12t+24) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-12t+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(12{t}^{2}-12t+12) \\\\  & =24t-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24t-12 & =0 \\\\ 24t & =12 \\\\ t & =\\frac{12}{24} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319022 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318665 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319022 1069 "q-345391" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319022 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319022 1071 "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t+12$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319022 1072 #uuid "cf0fa979-937b-587d-a91b-d1227e9d68bc" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319022 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-6t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-6t+6) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+t-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+t-6 & =0 \\\\ (t-2)(t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319023 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318798 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319023 1069 "q-345399" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319023 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319023 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x(6x-1){y}^{′}-3y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation given above, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x(6x-1){y}^{′} & =-\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -3y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319023 1072 #uuid "ff23238d-fed7-5e7b-a098-d5d0bcaea4b5" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319023 1074 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x(6x-1){y}^{′} & =-\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -3y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution.\nSolving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda +1)(2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}_{2}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/2}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319024 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318602 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319024 1069 "q-345405" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319024 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319024 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n8x{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-2y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 5{y}^{′} & =5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -2y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-2{a}_{n-1}){x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319024 1072 #uuid "85a1e731-266e-55a8-8160-c4ea4512b95d" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319024 1074 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 5{y}^{′} & =5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -2y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-2{a}_{n-1}){x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +5\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (8\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/8}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319025 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318498 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319025 1069 "q-345407" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319025 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319025 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n6x{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+3y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$