94139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319045 1071 "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{0}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319045 1072 #uuid "ec8fda44-370b-5d51-8569-7191b538acf7" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319045 1074 "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{0}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319046 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318577 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319046 1069 "q-345635" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319046 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319046 1071 "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319046 1072 #uuid "64fd9027-1cd8-5e75-a5d3-a8021a39b967" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319046 1074 "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319047 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318708 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319047 1069 "q-345643" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319047 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319047 1071 "![](images/63/6344388d587e3ee32acf6efcd0d20f501f005cdc2c9d7b245391a8158fb0d23f.png)\n\nFind the total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=2,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319047 1072 #uuid "461056ae-09e5-52f4-a80a-c9458666f4a4" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319047 1074 "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0\\in [-1,2],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [0,2]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x-2{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[{0}^{5}-(-1)^{5}]-\\frac{1}{2}[{0}^{4}-(-1)^{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[0-(-1)]-\\frac{1}{2}[0-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [0,2],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[{2}^{5}-{0}^{5}]+\\frac{1}{2}[{2}^{4}-{0}^{4}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[32]+\\frac{1}{2}[16] \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{5}+8 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}+\\frac{8}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319048 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318742 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319048 1069 "q-345644" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319048 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319048 1071 "Alex and Brooke agree to meet at the library. Alex arrives on time, but Brooke is late. After waiting for a while, Alex decides to leave and calls Brooke. They agree to head home instead.\n\nAlex travels north from the library at a speed of $6\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Brooke travels east away from the library. When Alex is $4\\,\\text{km}$ from the library, Brooke is $3\\,\\text{km}$ from the library. If the distance between them is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is Brooke’s speed?\n\nBrooke’s speed is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{ }\\text{km/h}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319048 1072 #uuid "16cd8a89-b31e-5ada-a39c-46883ef43ef8" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319048 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/46/46263b1e25bc520881d590ccab503ce0d6cd33e91288e81f1d678cde9a641214.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Brooke and the library,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Alex and the library,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between Alex and Brooke.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=4\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{2}+{4}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 9+16 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=6\\,\\text{km/h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=5\\,\\text{km/h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(4)(6) & =2(5)(6) \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+48 & =60 \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Brooke's speed is $2\\,\\text{km/h}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319049 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318561 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319049 1069 "q-345648" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319049 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319049 1071 "Find the total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+6x-5$ and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=7.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319049 1072 #uuid "eedff49e-0e8a-5193-afe0-b558ebadf6e5" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319049 1074 "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x-5 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5\\in [2,7],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=5.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=5$ to $x=7.$\n\n![](images/e1/e14a891e924a6911a0df962dc5c5b41c90b0c411de4ebe1f7e52ec1953e4124c.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [5,7]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{5}^{7}(-{x}^{2}+6x-5)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative.\n\nTherefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}(-{x}^{2}+6x-5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{2}^{5}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+6{\\int }_{2}^{5}x\\,\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{2}^{5}+3{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{2}^{5}-5x\\,{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[{5}^{3}-{2}^{3}]+3[{5}^{2}-{2}^{2}]-5[5-2] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(125-8)+3(25-4)-5(3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{117}{3}+63-15 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [5,7],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{5}^{7}(-{x}^{2}+6x-5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-6{\\int }_{5}^{7}x\\,\\text{d}x+5{\\int }_{5}^{7}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{5}^{7}-3{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{5}^{7}+5x\\,{|}_{5}^{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{7}^{3}-{5}^{3}]-3[{7}^{2}-{5}^{2}]+5[7-5] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(343-125)-3(49-25)+5(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{218}{3}-72+10 \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =9+\\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{59}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318811 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319050 1069 "q-34570" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319050 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319050 1071 "Find $k$, if $3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0$ and \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319050 1072 #uuid "4eddae70-87f3-5f4b-9148-8e92a4a81ace" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319050 1074 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+k[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ k[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ k[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get \n\n$$\n3k=-6\\,⟹\\,k=-2.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\ne [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $k$ such that $3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319051 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318951 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319051 1069 "q-34600" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319051 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319051 1071 "Find $l$, if $5{a}_{1}+l{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319051 1072 #uuid "c8dd7df5-a62a-5efa-a6ef-0c480856813f" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319051 1074 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $l$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}+l{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 5[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+l[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\ l[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ l[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that \n$l=3.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319052 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318558 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319052 1069 "q-34601" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319052 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319052 1071 "Find the value of ${x}_{3}-{x}_{4}$ given that  $6a-2x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319052 1072 #uuid "deec7ef1-a437-505b-aec9-2a0d7048c769" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319052 1074 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a-2x & =b \\\\ -2x & =b-6a \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}(b-6a) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -48 \\\\ -58\\end{aligned}] \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 24 \\\\ 29\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=24-29=-5.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319053 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318484 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319053 1069 "q-34602" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319053 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319053 1071 "Find $k$, if $8{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319053 1072 #uuid "3695980d-a1ca-52c3-a275-096dc38f4d3e" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319053 1074 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 8[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+k[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ k[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ k[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -48 \\\\ 32\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get \n\n$$\n-2k=24\\,⟹\\,k=-12.\n$$\n\nBut then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}]\\ne [\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -48 \\\\ 32\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $k$ such that \n $8{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319054 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318625 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319054 1069 "q-346028" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319054 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319054 1071 "The length of one leg of a right triangle increases at the rate of $5\\text{ m/s},$ while the length of the second leg is decreasing at the rate of $3\\text{ m/s}.$ How fast is the length of the hypotenuse changing when the length of the increasing leg is $9\\text{ m}$ and the length of the decreasing leg is $12\\text{ m}?$\n\nThe length of the hypotenuse is changing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319054 1072 #uuid "2eb6e001-d2df-5fc7-9255-0f6e2bb85276" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319054 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/40/40fd26817dc71b5ceee1861e20a812a1835fdb92d0ff7a903d05aacc2eca2c05.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the length of the increasing side,\n\n- $y$ is the length of the decreasing side,\n\n- $z$ is the length of the hypotenuse.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe know that $x=9\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=12\\,\\text{m}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {9}^{2}+{12}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 225 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =15\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $x=9\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=12\\,\\text{m},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $x=9\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=12\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(9)(5)+2(12)(-3) & =2(15)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 18 & =30\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the hypotenuse is changing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319055 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318678 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319055 1069 "q-346032" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319055 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319055 1071 "Two snowplows are approaching a maintenance station $T$ along perpendicular roads. Snowplow $E$ drives due south toward $T$ at $24\\,\\text{mph},$ while snowplow $F$ drives due west toward $T$ at $10\\,\\text{mph}.$ When snowplow $F$ is $6\\,\\text{miles}$ from $T$ and snowplow $E$ is $8\\,\\text{miles}$ from $T$, how fast is the distance between the snowplows decreasing? Round your answer to the **nearest integer** and assume that the rate of decreasing distance is **positive**.\n\nThe speed of convergence of the two snowplows is approximately\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mph}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319055 1072 #uuid "86ac752b-6fd8-5a74-81ec-3d9769e9b6d6" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319055 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the situation.\n\n![](images/11/1109f4736ddfa81344d545802438588967aa18aac379c75a6b7be58688fb931f.png)\n\nIn the diagram:\n\n- $x$ is the distance between snowplow $F$ and the maintenance station $T$\n\n- $y$ is the distance between snowplow $E$ and the maintenance station $T$\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the snowplows.\n\nBoth snowplows are moving toward $T$, so the leg rates are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{dt} & =-10\\text{ }\\text{mph} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dt} & =-24\\text{ }\\text{mph}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, compute $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {6}^{2}+{8}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 36+64 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =100 \\\\ z & =10\\text{ }\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and substitute the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\frac{dx}{dt}+2y\\frac{dy}{dt} & =2z\\frac{dz}{dt} \\\\ 2(6)(-10)+2(8)(-24) & =2(10)\\frac{dz}{dt} \\\\ -120-384 & =20\\frac{dz}{dt} \\\\ -504 & =20\\frac{dz}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dz}{dt} & ≈-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nSo, the speed of convergence of the two snowplows is approximately $25\\text{ }\\text{mph}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318816 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319056 1069 "q-346036" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319056 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319056 1071 "The graph of the function $f(x)$ passes through $(1,-4)$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=2.$ Determine $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n$L(x)=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319056 1072 #uuid "73fb6981-d54b-5c86-b585-a5ad9cb2a2fb" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319056 1074 "Since the graph of $f(x)$ passes through $(1,-4)$, we have that $f(1)=-4.$ Therefore, the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $a=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1) \\\\  & =-4+2(x-1) \\\\  & =-4+2x-2 \\\\  & =2x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318454 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319057 1069 "q-346037" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319057 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319057 1071 "The graph of the function $f(x)$ passes through $(2,-1)$ and ${f}^{′}(2)=4.$ Determine $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2.$\n\n$L(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319057 1072 #uuid "b29f7675-f6de-5ca8-84a7-0d9e799ca83c" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319057 1074 "Since the graph of $f(x)$ passes through $(2,-1)$, we have that $f(2)=-1.$ Therefore, the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $a=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2) \\\\  & =-1+4(x-2) \\\\  & =-1+4x-8 \\\\  & =4x-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318917 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319058 1069 "q-346041" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319058 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319058 1071 "Suppose that $f(-4)=1$ and ${f}^{′}(-4)=6.$ Using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-4,$ estimate the value of $f(-3.8).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(-3.8)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319058 1072 #uuid "e03e29d0-50e9-528b-9c71-b52ed36384c2" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319058 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=-4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-4)+{f}^{′}(-4)(x-(-4)) \\\\  & =1+6(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-3.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-3.8.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3.8) & ≈L(-3.8) \\\\  & =1+6(-3.8+4) \\\\  & =1+6(0.2) \\\\  & =1+1.2 \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318636 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319059 1069 "q-346043" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319059 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319059 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{6}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4,$ calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{6}(5f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{6}(5f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319059 1072 #uuid "87c8f038-8768-5c99-a9bb-bf53b5a8b948" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319059 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{6}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}(5f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(-4)\\,+\\,x\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =-20\\,+\\,(6-0) \\\\  & =-20\\,+\\,6 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318695 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319060 1069 "q-346048" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319060 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319060 1071 "An engineer measures the amount of fuel $F$, in liters, remaining in a generator. The following table shows the rate at which fuel is being consumed.\n\n| **$t\\,\\text{(hours)}$** | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{(liters/hour)}$ | $-3.1$ | $-3.5$ | $-4.0$ | $-4.4$ |\n\nAt $t=4,$ the generator contained $62$ liters of fuel. Use the tangent line approximation of $F$ at time $t=4$ to estimate the amount of fuel remaining at $t=4.6.$  Do not round the answer.\n\nThe amount of fuel remaining at $t=4.6$ hours is estimated to be\n{{field-1}}\nliters." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319060 1072 #uuid "968b98d4-c3b7-5ca5-a63d-cddc410d9d66" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319060 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $F(t)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =F(a)+{F}^{′}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =F(4)+{F}^{′}(4)(t-4) \\\\  & =62-3.5(t-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $F(4.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $t=4.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4.6) & ≈L(4.6) \\\\  & =62-3.5(4.6-4) \\\\  & =62-3.5(0.6) \\\\  & =62-2.1 \\\\  & =59.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe amount of fuel remaining at $t=4.6$ hours is estimated to be $59.9$ liters." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318472 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319061 1069 "q-346050" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319061 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319061 1071 "A surveyor stands $15\\text{ ft}$ away from the base of a radio tower. An elevator inside the tower moves straight up at a constant speed of $20\\text{ ft/s}.$ How fast is the distance between the surveyor and the elevator changing when the elevator is $20\\text{ ft}$ above the ground?\n\nThe distance between the surveyor and the elevator is\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319061 1072 #uuid "064c7dea-229d-541a-b55e-0089485e7b3f" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319061 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/7a/7a6f5b05bc0bd89d01b1b521fa35f4bc613e532a06ce04fea26323e6c5dbbafa.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the surveyor and the base of the tower,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the elevator and the ground,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the surveyor and the elevator.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=15\\,\\text{ft}$ and $y=20\\,\\text{ft}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {15}^{2}+{20}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 625 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =25\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=20\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$\n\n- the distance between the surveyor and the base of the tower remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(15)(0)+2(20)(20) & =2(25)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 800 & =50\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the surveyor and the elevator is $16\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319062 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318572 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319062 1069 "q-346051" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319062 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319062 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{6}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-7,$ calculate \n${\\int }_{1}^{6}(2f(x)-1)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{6}(2f(x)-1)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319062 1072 #uuid "cae281eb-8a02-5639-a428-25f305353613" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319062 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{6}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}(2f(x)-1)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(7)\\,-\\,x\\,{|}_{1}^{6} \\\\  & =14\\,-\\,(6-1) \\\\  & =14\\,-\\,5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319063 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318641 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319063 1069 "q-346054" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319063 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319063 1071 "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{3}-1$ and $f(-1)=2.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ to estimate the value of $f(-0.6).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(-0.6)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319063 1072 #uuid "e5548b79-a226-52a8-a01d-075439687ed3" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319063 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-1)+{f}^{′}(-1)(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(-1)=2,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{3}-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(-1)=(-1)^{3}-1=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=2-2(x+1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(-0.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-0.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-0.6) & ≈L(-0.6) \\\\  & =2-2(-0.6+1) \\\\  & =2-2(0.4) \\\\  & =2-0.8 \\\\  & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319064 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318430 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319064 1069 "q-346057" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319064 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319064 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{4}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3,$ calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{4}(2f(x)+x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}(2f(x)+x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319064 1072 #uuid "992d58de-09a2-5be8-96ce-d8f85bd901f5" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319064 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{4}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(2f(x)+x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(3)\\,+\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =6\\,+\\,\\frac{16-1}{2} \\\\  & =6\\,+\\,\\frac{15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319065 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318846 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319065 1069 "q-346059" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319065 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319065 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6,$ calculate ${\\int }_{-3}^{0}(5x-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{0}(5x-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319065 1072 #uuid "1bb6fe16-db8c-5c01-bc00-6879dd803cc4" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319065 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}(5x-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{-3}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{-3}^{0}\\,-\\,(-6) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{0-9}{2}\\,+\\,6 \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{2}\\,+\\,6 \\\\  & =-\\frac{33}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318894 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319066 1069 "q-346061" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319066 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{6}^{-6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1,$ calculate ${\\int }_{-6}^{6}(3x-9f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{6}(3x-9f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319066 1072 #uuid "41f66289-6d32-564b-b35d-6792bee8309d" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319066 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{6}^{-6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{6}(3x-9f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-6}^{6}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,-\\,9{\\int }_{-6}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{-6}^{6}\\,-\\,9(-1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{6}^{2}-(-6)^{2}}{2}\\,+\\,9 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0\\,+\\,9 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318520 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319067 1069 "q-346074" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319067 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319067 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{8}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}{8}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319067 1072 #uuid "3ce119ff-d188-56bb-9854-5cb613b7f364" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319067 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{8}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{8}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\cdot {8}^{x}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}({8}^{2}-{8}^{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}(64-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318898 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319068 1069 "q-346078" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319068 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319068 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319068 1072 #uuid "73f7baa1-aec8-509d-94ff-ae56b865d2b8" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319068 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={e}^{x}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318534 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319069 1069 "q-346080" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319069 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319069 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{2}^{5}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{5}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319069 1072 #uuid "5116d7f8-134c-559d-8c66-74609b4899f0" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319069 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={e}^{x}\\,{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & ={e}^{5}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{5}-{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318847 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319070 1069 "q-346081" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319070 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319070 1072 #uuid "af8d7c5c-930c-59d3-b49e-ff7666f63383" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319070 1074 "If we let $u=\\frac{\\pi x}{4},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318551 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319071 1069 "q-346084" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319071 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319071 1072 #uuid "58a433e2-084f-559f-bd64-1862ef8ffdc9" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319071 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318934 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319072 1069 "q-346090" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319072 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319072 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319072 1072 #uuid "8ad5354f-415f-5373-a369-188a90efa535" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319072 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =cot\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-cot\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318954 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319073 1069 "q-346092" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319073 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319073 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319073 1072 #uuid "f3de3131-f650-58c5-a909-7a85b83b3f4a" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319073 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318603 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319074 1069 "q-346094" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319074 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319074 1072 #uuid "a00832dd-966d-5a5a-8da4-9f3ee1f87bfb" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319074 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}-(-2) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318481 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318635 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318719 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319075 1069 "q-346160" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319075 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319075 1071 "![](images/3b/3b53e88e4f93985ca999c7fd9d4e3fe3591afe10e7fbe499a303d22bfb0ce6ea.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}+1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $=$ | $\\int $ | {{field-2}} | $\\text{d}x$ |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319075 1072 #uuid "0b951b0a-f15d-5539-a6a1-13a6d202a4ef" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319075 1074 "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}+1$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=-3$ and $x=2.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}+1$ between the lower limit $x=-3$ and the upper limit $x=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-3}^{2}\\sqrt{x+4}+1\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319076 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318462 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319076 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318753 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319076 1069 "q-346179" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319076 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319076 1071 "Express ${\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{3}+2($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319076 1072 #uuid "18197a01-d08b-5472-bcd0-342b90f62fdf" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319076 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1,$ the lower bound is $a=0,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[({(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)}^{3}+2(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)+1)\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318740 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318867 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319077 1069 "q-346180" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319077 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319077 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[({2}^{\\,2+3k/n}+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319077 1072 #uuid "aad0a1ec-7573-56fe-9f5c-ec39ca09c20a" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319077 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={2}^{\\,{x}_{k}}+1.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}({2}^{x}+1)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[({2}^{\\,2+3k/n}+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={2}^{x}+1\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319078 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318493 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319078 1069 "q-346181" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319078 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319078 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{6}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{113}{9},$ calculate ${\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9f(x)+8x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9f(x)+8x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319078 1072 #uuid "e8c498fe-e615-5851-9e66-78de56e7b50e" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319078 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{6}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{113}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9f(x)+8x)\\,\\text{d}x & =9{\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,8{\\int }_{-3}^{6}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9(-\\frac{113}{9})\\,+\\,8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{-3}^{6} \\\\  & =-113\\,+\\,8\\cdot \\frac{{6}^{2}-(-3)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-113\\,+\\,8\\cdot \\frac{27}{2} \\\\  & =-113\\,+\\,108 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318495 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319079 1069 "q-346186" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319079 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{2}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=3$ and ${\\int }_{7}^{11}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-10,$ \nthen what is ${\\int }_{2}^{11}(2-6f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{11}(2-6f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319079 1072 #uuid "b603f0ce-2c69-5d46-8220-cdd905b06247" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319079 1074 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{7}^{11}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,{\\int }_{2}^{11}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information gives\n\n$$\n3+(-10)\\,=\\,{\\int }_{2}^{11}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,⇒\\,{\\int }_{2}^{11}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-7.\n$$\n\nFinally, compute the desired integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{11}(2-6f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{2}^{11}1\\,\\text{d}x\\,-\\,6{\\int }_{2}^{11}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot x{|}_{2}^{11}\\,-\\,6(-7) \\\\  & =2(11-2)+42 \\\\  & =18+42 \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318664 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319080 1069 "q-346187" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319080 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319080 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1$ and ${\\int }_{6}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=11,$ \nthen what is ${\\int }_{4}^{10}(6-3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{4}^{10}(6-3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319080 1072 #uuid "d7d55c58-0a18-53f9-953d-6a757a935d02" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319080 1074 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{6}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{4}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information gives\n\n$$\n(-1)+11={\\int }_{4}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,⇒\\,{\\int }_{4}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=10.\n$$\n\nFinally, compute the desired integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{10}(6-3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{4}^{10}1\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{4}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot x{|}_{4}^{10}-3(10) \\\\  & =6(10-4)-30 \\\\  & =36-30 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319081 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318673 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319081 1069 "q-346188" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319081 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319081 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-5$ then what is ${\\int }_{1}^{6}(2f(x)+4)\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{6}(2f(x)+4)\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319081 1072 #uuid "db436a88-52f6-54e4-9c5a-67c578368efd" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319081 1074 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information gives\n\n$$\n2+(-5)={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,⇒\\,{\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, compute the desired integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}(2f(x)+4)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(-3)+4\\cdot x\\,{|}_{1}^{6} \\\\  & =-6+4(6-1) \\\\  & =-6+20 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319082 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318839 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319082 1069 "q-346189" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319082 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319082 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=7$ and ${\\int }_{4}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ then what is ${\\int }_{0}^{8}(3+f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{8}(3+f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319082 1072 #uuid "208151a8-b3bf-5291-8094-fc9f0c810f67" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319082 1074 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{4}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7+1={\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,⇒\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, compute the desired integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}(3+f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot x\\,{|}_{0}^{8}+8 \\\\  & =3(8-0)+8 \\\\  & =24+8 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319083 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318569 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319083 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318960 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319083 1069 "q-346190" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319083 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319083 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[5{(2+\\frac{4k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319083 1072 #uuid "91c3be0f-8f8e-52a1-9627-d3cf3af82a0a" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319083 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=5{x}_{k}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[5{(2+\\frac{4k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=6.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318479 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319084 1069 "q-346195" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319084 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319084 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$ and ${\\int }_{7}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5,$ then what is ${\\int }_{3}^{10}(3f(x)-4)\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{10}(3f(x)-4)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319084 1072 #uuid "e8b10bff-e1bb-5bf7-aa89-87d2bca90f8b" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319084 1074 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{7}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,{\\int }_{3}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information gives\n\n$$\n0+5\\,=\\,{\\int }_{3}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,⇒\\,{\\int }_{3}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5.\n$$\n\nFinally, compute the desired integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{10}(3f(x)-4)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{3}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,-\\,4{\\int }_{3}^{10}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(5)\\,-\\,4\\cdot x{|}_{3}^{10} \\\\  & =15\\,-\\,4(10-3) \\\\  & =15-28 \\\\  & =-13\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319085 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318754 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319085 1069 "q-346197" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319085 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319085 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ and ${\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=3,$ \nthen what is ${\\int }_{-2}^{6}(6f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{6}(6f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319085 1072 #uuid "37dd94ec-757b-5586-b66e-3f8ade17d0a5" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319085 1074 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information gives\n\n$$\n1+3={\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,⇒\\,{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4.\n$$\n\nFinally, compute the desired integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{6}(6f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-2}^{6}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6(4)+x\\,{|}_{-2}^{6} \\\\  & =24+(6-(-2)) \\\\  & =24+8 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319086 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318795 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319086 1069 "q-3462" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319086 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319086 1071 "A farmer needs to enclose a field with a rectangular fence. He has $500\\,\\text{ft}$ of fencing material. A large building is on one side of the field so this side will not require any fencing. Determine the largest possible area that can be enclosed by the rectangular fence." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319086 1072 #uuid "396b5eb6-ef05-5b8e-aee5-ad9f1e0e441d" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319086 1074 "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](images/05/050111089f76ecb6d2b278fce0d1faa71635bc07d107d2539cf5f4bb28a32476.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $500\\,\\text{ft}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nx+2y=500.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =500 \\\\ x & =500-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=500-2y$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(500-2y)y \\\\ A & =500y-2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(y)=500-4y,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 500-4y & =0 \\\\ 4y & =500 \\\\ y & =\\frac{500}{4} \\\\ y & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $y=125$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(y)=-4\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(125)=-4<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(125)$ is negative, we conclude that $y=125$ is indeed a maximum of $A(y).$\n\nFinally, when $y=125$, we have \n\n$$\ny=500-2(125)=250.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the field with the largest area are $x=250$ and $y=125,$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nA=xy=250\\cdot 125=31\\,250.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318556 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318644 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319087 1069 "q-346201" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319087 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319087 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{((-1+\\frac{3k}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319087 1072 #uuid "c0de2e03-fab0-563b-b613-a64d3498b437" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319087 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-(-1)}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=-1+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=({x}_{k}+8{)}^{1/3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(x+8{)}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{((-1+\\frac{3k}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+8{)}^{1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=2.\n$$" 13194139533419