7592186319092 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{5}^{-1}7\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=14$ and ${\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319092 1072 #uuid "bdf4ff7d-2b10-50e1-9730-8cf7f3ede29a" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319092 1074 "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{-1}7\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =14 \\\\ 7{\\int }_{5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =14 \\\\ -7{\\int }_{-1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =14 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319093 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318453 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319093 1069 "q-346225" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319093 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319093 1071 "Let $f$ and $g$ be continuous functions such that ${\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=9,\\,\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{2}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-4,$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{8}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x=10.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319093 1072 #uuid "c2856014-8050-53b5-8077-c04d2dfe3bd5" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319093 1074 "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{2}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-4 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the adjacent intervals rule and substituting known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{8}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the sum rule for definite integrals,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(9)+(-8) \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =19-{\\int }_{3}^{8}(3f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =19-10 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318944 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319094 1069 "q-346226" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319094 1071 "Let $f$ and $g$ be continuous functions such that ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-4,\\,\\,{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{2}{3}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6,$ and ${\\int }_{-1}^{3}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x=-20.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319094 1072 #uuid "38e97327-3fa6-5865-86a2-b52a48dcf2d7" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319094 1074 "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{2}{3}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =6 \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the adjacent intervals rule and substituting known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,+{\\int }_{-1}^{3}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the sum rule for definite integrals,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{-3}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(-4)-4(9) \\\\  & =-46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =-46-{\\int }_{-1}^{3}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-46-(-20) \\\\  & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318700 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319095 1069 "q-346240" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319095 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319095 1071 "A contractor has $18\\,in$ of trim to mark out a rectangular opening. Find the maximum area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$ that can be marked out with the trim. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,{in}^{2}$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319095 1072 #uuid "75f6ce5d-5587-5359-99a4-e02f7c9a6617" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319095 1074 "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectagular area.\n\n![](images/59/59c6b4e09015898c1cd77d96fba2bc0dca037ef7462a15f90765257888e3331d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $18.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+2y=18.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =18 \\\\ y & =9-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=9-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(9-x) \\\\ A & =9x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=9-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 9-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =9 \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{9}{2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{9}{2})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{9}{2})$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{9}{2}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{9}{2}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=9-\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{9}{2}\\,in$ and $y=\\frac{9}{2}\\,in,$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{max}}=xy=\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}=20.25\\,{in}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318611 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319096 1069 "q-346249" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319096 1071 "A rectangle has an area of $450\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$ What is the minimum possible perimeter ${P}_{\\text{min}}$ of the rectangle? Do not round your answer.\n\n${P}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319096 1072 #uuid "2af97986-d4fc-5710-a371-6bfcbb64bfc8" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319096 1074 "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](images/cd/cd9fa71ea72045881c25c80de4f6d8b294b60d92ea4e4f27170d68849ce31431.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2(x+y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $450\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=450.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{450}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{450}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{900}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{900}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{900}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =900 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =450 \\\\ x & =15\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=15\\sqrt{2}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{1800}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{1800}{(15\\sqrt{2})^{3}}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{2})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=15\\sqrt{2}$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=15\\sqrt{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{450}{15\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{30}{\\sqrt{2}}=15\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=15\\sqrt{2}$ and $y=15\\sqrt{2}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $15\\sqrt{2}\\,cm.$\n\nFinally, the minimum possible perimeter is\n\n$$\n{P}_{\\text{min}}=4\\cdot 15\\sqrt{2}=60\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318648 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319097 1069 "q-346253" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319097 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319097 1071 "A rectangle has an area of $675\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.$ What is the minimum possible perimeter ${P}_{\\text{min}}$ of the rectangle? Do not round your answer.\n\n${P}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319097 1072 #uuid "7477e8bb-dec7-5056-9ba5-eac2a2896c95" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319097 1074 "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](images/cf/cf3d29b794698ff529da4c21f6fd02b3487d1650ec84b3c1f992280616f7701d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2(x+y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $675\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=675.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{675}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{675}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{1350}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{1350}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{1350}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =1350 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =675 \\\\ x & =15\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=15\\sqrt{3}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{2700}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{2700}{(15\\sqrt{3})^{3}}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{3})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=15\\sqrt{3}$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=15\\sqrt{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{675}{15\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{45}{\\sqrt{3}}=15\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=15\\sqrt{3}$ and $y=15\\sqrt{3}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $15\\sqrt{3}\\,ft.$\n\nFinally, the minimum possible perimeter is\n\n$$\n{P}_{\\text{min}}=4\\cdot 15\\sqrt{3}=60\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318929 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319098 1069 "q-346255" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319098 1071 "A farmer wants to build a rectangular livestock pen of area $648\\,{ft}^{2}.$ One side of the pen is adjacent to a barn, so that side does not require fencing. Find $x+y$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the pen that minimize the amount of fencing required.\n\n$x+y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319098 1072 #uuid "f33e4009-fc1f-57ea-a385-f971677ad6e8" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319098 1074 "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](images/83/8377f61340020a602e6226932a14fb56ceb803bc9efff05223682f9cca1cf91e.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=x+2y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $648\\,{ft}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=648.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{648}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =x+2(\\frac{648}{x}) \\\\  & =x+\\frac{1296}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{1296}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{1296}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1296 \\\\ x & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=36$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{2592}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(36)=\\frac{2592}{(36)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{18}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(36)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=36$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{648}{36}=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the lot that has the least perimeter are $x=36\\,ft$ and $y=18\\,ft,$ and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx+y=36+18=54\\,ft.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318736 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319099 1069 "q-346256" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319099 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319099 1071 "A park wants to build a rectangular skating area of area $1250\\,{yd}^{2}.$ One side of the area is adjacent to a wall, so that side does not require fencing. Find $x+y$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the area that minimize the amount of fencing required.\n\n$x+y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{yd}$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319099 1072 #uuid "970cce65-2ceb-5bbc-b96e-29b0275ee6d7" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319099 1074 "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](images/f1/f1ce707b311c320fe7dbf3ba2ad1daff6669a67ab1d7652fc8d98065c9ae747d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=x+2y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $1250\\,{yd}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=1250.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1250}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =x+2(\\frac{1250}{x}) \\\\  & =x+\\frac{2500}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2500}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{2500}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2500 \\\\ x & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=50$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{5000}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(50)=\\frac{5000}{(50)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{25}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(50)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=50$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=50,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1250}{50}=25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the lot that has the least perimeter are $x=50\\,yd$ and $y=25\\,yd,$ and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx+y=50+25=75\\,yd.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318586 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319100 1069 "q-346368" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319100 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319100 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319100 1072 #uuid "ec7ce12f-cbf4-5c03-8053-383589db4d45" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319100 1074 "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{4},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $2\\pi /3$ | $2\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u))\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =4(-1+2) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318705 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319101 1069 "q-346390" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319101 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319101 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1/2}^{3/2}4x\\sqrt{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319101 1072 #uuid "7fd825e3-0d14-5ed8-a4b9-9f4979a0fb9f" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319101 1074 "Let $u=2x+1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=\\frac{1}{2}(u-1).$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $-1/2$ | $3/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $0$ | $4$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/2}^{3/2}4x\\sqrt{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(u-1)\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{(5/2)}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{(3/2)}{u}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {4}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3/2})-(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {0}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{5}-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{112}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318606 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319102 1069 "q-346393" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319102 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319102 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{3x+1}{\\sqrt{x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319102 1072 #uuid "11e654cd-30e9-5558-8788-bee1abdebb50" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319102 1074 "Let $u=x+3.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=u-3.$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{3x+1}{\\sqrt{x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{3(u-3)+1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{3u-8}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{9}(3{u}^{1/2}-8{u}^{-1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{(3/2)}{u}^{3/2}-\\frac{8}{(1/2)}{u}^{1/2})|}_{4}^{9} \\\\  & ={(2{u}^{3/2}-16{u}^{1/2})|}_{4}^{9} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {9}^{3/2}-16\\cdot {9}^{1/2})-(2\\cdot {4}^{3/2}-16\\cdot {4}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 27-16\\cdot 3)-(2\\cdot 8-16\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =(54-48)-(16-32) \\\\  & =6-(-16) \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318594 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319103 1069 "q-346400" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319103 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319103 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\cdot {3}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319103 1072 #uuid "03065e0a-3e2d-519b-9716-a27401bbed9d" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319103 1074 "Let's introduce the new variable $u={x}^{2}.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the new limits of integration:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\cdot {3}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\,|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}({3}^{1}-{3}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}(3-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318526 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319104 1069 "q-346401" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319104 1071 "![](images/94/94ef52e454545335132d467773c0b274296a7cb3758ae9fae3242876c8063f5f.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{2}-3y$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=2$ and $y=4,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319104 1072 #uuid "54b385aa-9795-5ad7-b92b-a662f02e6b0a" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319104 1074 "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{2}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,-\\,3{\\int }_{2}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{2}^{4}\\,-\\,3\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(\\frac{64}{3}-\\frac{8}{3})\\,-\\,3(8-2) \\\\  & =14-18 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $|-4|=4.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318471 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319105 1069 "q-346428" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319105 1071 "![](images/25/25f1ddf290a96f1ef7393d33583b2f519bf8b5c8e66166240345a611d10fba96.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded between the curve $x=5{y}^{4}+3$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319105 1072 #uuid "a8ea79a2-e70f-57e2-b0dc-e9a074300f6b" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319105 1074 "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(5{y}^{4}+3)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y+3{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+3y\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =({1}^{5}-{0}^{5})+3(1-0) \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318507 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319106 1069 "q-346434" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319106 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319106 1072 #uuid "b8d4df78-0e9a-508b-ae35-e42755fe5bc0" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319106 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e$ | ${e}^{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot {u}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}({4}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =\\frac{75}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318528 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319107 1069 "q-346435" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319107 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{4}^{32}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{{log}_{2}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319107 1072 #uuid "cd8052bf-7195-55ef-bdc6-284e7fea5262" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319107 1074 "Let $u={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot \\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $32$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{32}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{{log}_{2}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{32}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{log}_{2}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{u}\\,{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318887 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319108 1069 "q-346442" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319108 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319108 1071 "| $x$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $7$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $4$ | $2$ | $k$ | $6$ |\n\nThe table above gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[-1,11]$ with a regular step size. If the right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $96$, then what must be the value of $k?$ \n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319108 1072 #uuid "1ea325bd-32b0-55f1-9bf5-12997a89e2cb" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319108 1074 "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)+f(7)+f(11)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $7$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $k$ | $6$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(2+k+6)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =(k+8)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4k+32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A$ must be  $96,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4k+32 & =96 \\\\ 4k & =96-32 \\\\ k & =\\frac{64}{4} \\\\ k & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318853 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319109 1069 "q-346451" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319109 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\frac{2}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319109 1072 #uuid "0ba9633b-5871-51f9-8aee-a3691915151d" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319109 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $9{x}^{2}=(3x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\frac{2}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\frac{1}{1+(3x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318938 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319110 1069 "q-346457" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319110 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319110 1071 "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[10,16].$\n\n| $x$ | $10$ | $12$ | $13.5$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $7$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area $A$ under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[10,16].$ \n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319110 1072 #uuid "89164dc6-dacc-5e89-922a-cc492105d5f9" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319110 1074 "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈f(12)Δ{x}_{1}+f(13.5)Δ{x}_{2}+f(16)Δ{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $10$ | $12$ | $13.5$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $-$ | $2$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 1.5+3\\cdot 2.5 \\\\  & =4+6+7.5 \\\\  & =17.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318882 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319111 1069 "q-346458" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319111 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{100-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319111 1072 #uuid "684a7fc3-f685-5be8-90e1-52ef15ddd822" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319111 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{100-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{100\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/100)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{10}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/100}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/10)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{10})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{10}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=10\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{100-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/10)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 10\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318613 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318666 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318962 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319112 1069 "q-346467" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319112 1071 "Consider the area under the curve $y=(10-2x)^{2}$ over the interval $[2,4]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$The estimated area under the curve $y=(10-2x)^{2}$ over the interval $[2,4]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319112 1072 #uuid "326f5dcc-33da-504b-935b-affa41e78ccc" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319112 1074 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/1c/1cb4c2c69c93117bd5eed39322059a3c0af84d16b293ccfc053c677ac0b6127d.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2.5)+f(3)+f(3.5)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $2.5$ | $3$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $25$ | $16$ | $9$ | $4$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+16+9+4)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =54\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{decreasing}$ over the interval $[2,4],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319113 1069 "q-346471" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319113 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319113 1071 "Find the area $A$ bounded between the curve $x=24-6{y}^{2}$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=-3$ and $y=-2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319113 1072 #uuid "a2e5fb88-b6e1-5248-b404-e31c318ad47c" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319113 1074 "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-2}(24-6{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =24{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,-\\,6{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =24y\\,{|}_{-3}^{-2}-2{y}^{3}\\,{|}_{-3}^{-2} \\\\  & =24(-2-(-3))-2(-8-(-27)) \\\\  & =24-38 \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $|-14|=14.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318684 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319114 1069 "q-346495" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319114 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319114 1072 #uuid "0a15a17d-ca7d-59f1-8794-23e0f15ee7e4" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319114 1074 "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+3\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+3)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=8D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-(4{x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(-(4{\\pi }^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +8\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )-(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+8(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(4{\\pi }^{2}+3-8)-(-3+8) \\\\  & =4{\\pi }^{2}-5-5 \\\\  & =4{\\pi }^{2}-10\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319115 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318543 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319115 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318759 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319115 1069 "q-346519" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319115 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319115 1071 "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$artanh\\,(${{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319115 1072 #uuid "198c9f13-854c-5a78-a110-5897652614aa" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319115 1074 "$$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C,\n$$\n\nthe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3/5} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})-4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319116 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318455 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319116 1069 "q-346521" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319116 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319116 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319116 1072 #uuid "4b4d889c-3125-509c-8bd0-37836a63b86f" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319116 1074 "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}-{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,(x){|}_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}-{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}x(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3}}{4})\\,(\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4})\\,(\\frac{1}{4})]-{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3})-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-x{|}_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2{e}^{3}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319117 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318645 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319117 1069 "q-346522" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319117 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319117 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319117 1072 #uuid "6f5d1a83-72d6-5baf-8989-3f4b0012df34" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319117 1074 "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}}\\,\\text{d}x=4{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2-12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2 \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319118 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318933 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319118 1069 "q-346529" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319118 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319118 1071 "Calculate $F(2)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$F(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319118 1072 #uuid "4464a4f1-fd10-5a71-ae96-01dbf0286299" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319118 1074 "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF(2)={\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nNow, computing the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{0}^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{4}-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318535 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319119 1069 "q-346530" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319119 1071 "Calculate $F(2)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}({t}^{4}-t)\\,\\text{d}t.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$F(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319119 1072 #uuid "5f2a42ce-7bdd-5df7-a90b-4527797f0f5c" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319119 1074 "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF(2)={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{4}-t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nNow, computing the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{4}-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-(\\frac{{0}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{32}{5}-\\frac{4}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319120 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318877 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319120 1069 "q-346534" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319120 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319120 1071 "Calculate $F(9)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n$F(9)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319120 1072 #uuid "82a63a8c-7b40-5f0a-8386-79160b61b0f2" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319120 1074 "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=9,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(9)={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(9) & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{9}{t}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{t}^{1/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =12({9}^{1/2}-{1}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =12(3-1) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319121 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318945 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319121 1069 "q-346545" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319121 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319121 1071 "Calculate $F(1)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}10{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n$F(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319121 1072 #uuid "cb6432c8-fd42-50ae-956e-a119db585dcf" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319121 1074 "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=1,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(1)={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2}{e}^{2t+1}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =5({e}^{2(1)+1}-{e}^{2(0)+1}) \\\\  & =5({e}^{3}-e).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319122 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318822 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319122 1069 "q-346546" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319122 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319122 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(14{cos}^{2}⁡x-5{x}^{2}+14{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319122 1072 #uuid "c916ee0c-43c4-5e1c-ab73-9f1719924580" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319122 1074 "We use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(14{cos}^{2}⁡x-5{x}^{2}+14{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(14({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(14\\cdot 1-5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(14-5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(14x-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[14\\pi -\\frac{5}{3}{\\pi }^{3}]-[14\\cdot 0-\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}] \\\\  & =14\\pi -\\frac{5{\\pi }^{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318478 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319123 1069 "q-346553" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319123 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319123 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cos}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319123 1072 #uuid "eb1a86d3-f725-5234-baee-3e7bfe9e8077" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319123 1074 "We use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cos}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0-0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318931 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319124 1069 "q-346555" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319124 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319124 1071 "Calculate $F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n$F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319124 1072 #uuid "9846049f-0423-59a8-8569-7eab74f54e97" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319124 1074 "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318782 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319125 1069 "q-346569" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319125 1071 "Fuel is being pumped into a storage tank at a rate of $8t$ gallons per hour. If at time $t=0$ the tank already contains $12$ gallons of fuel, write an expression for $V(t),$ the total amount of fuel in the tank at time $t,$ where $t$ is measured in hours.\n\n$V(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319125 1072 #uuid "86699991-5bdb-5013-a56a-c492ef135e33" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319125 1074 "Let $f(t)=8t$ represent the rate at which fuel enters the tank, and let $V(T)$ be the total amount of fuel in the tank after $T$ hours.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nV(T)=V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(T) & =V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12+{\\int }_{0}^{T}8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={12+(4{t}^{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =12+4{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nV(t)=12+4{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318956 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319126 1069 "q-346571" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319126 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319126 1071 "Thermal energy is being added to a device at a rate of $3t$ joules per second. If at time $t=0$ the device already contains $15$ joules of energy, write an expression for $E(t),$ the total energy in the device at time $t,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\n$E(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319126 1072 #uuid "c755b337-b4d4-5ea7-9e4c-878004ac0d92" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319126 1074 "Let $f(t)=3t$ represent the rate at which energy enters the device, and let $E(T)$ be the total energy in the device after $T$ seconds.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nE(T)=E(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(T) & =E(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =15+{\\int }_{0}^{T}3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={15+(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =15+\\frac{3{T}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nE(t)=15+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318704 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319127 1069 "q-346575" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319127 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(1-{cot}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319127 1072 #uuid "2bb34ddd-c2ee-57af-a0a2-9fb98a895f33" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319127 1074 "From the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =x-\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{cot}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ {cot}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(1-{cot}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(1-{csc}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(2-{csc}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318735 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319128 1069 "q-346576" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319128 1071 "The total number of pages of a document printed $t$ minutes after printing begins increases at a rate of $\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}.$ If, at $t=0$ minutes, the printer had already produced $40$ pages, how many pages will the printer have produced after $2$ minutes?\n\nAfter $2$ minutes the printer has produced\n{{field-1}} pages." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319128 1072 #uuid "b23fa132-091d-593b-a542-6eca17f50321" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319128 1074 "Let $a(t)=\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}$ be the rate at which the number of pages produced is changing, and let $A(T)$ be the number of pages produced $T$ minutes after printing begins. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{T}\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{t+1}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{T+1})-(-\\frac{18}{1}) \\\\  & =40-\\frac{18}{T+1}+18 \\\\  & =58-\\frac{18}{T+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of pages produced $T=2$ minutes after printing begins, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nA(2)=40-\\frac{18}{2+1}+18=52.\n$$\n\nThus, after $2$ minutes, the printer has produced $52$ pages." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318774 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319129 1069 "q-346579" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319129 1071 "The total mass, in kilograms, of compost produced $t$ days after a process begins increases at a rate of $7{e}^{t}.$ If, at $t=0$ days, the process had already produced $5$ kilograms of compost, how much compost will the process have produced after $2$ days?\n\nAfter $2$ days, the process has produced\n{{field-1}} kilograms of compost." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319129 1072 #uuid "894bc11a-d9f0-5265-bd34-e9b27c037905" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319129 1074 "Let $a(t)=7{e}^{t}$ be the rate at which the amount of compost produced is changing and let $A(T)$ be the amount of compost produced $T$ days after the process begins. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5+{\\int }_{0}^{T}7{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5+(7{e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =5+(7{e}^{T})-(7{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =5+7{e}^{T}-7 \\\\  & =7{e}^{T}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the amount of compost produced $T=2$ days after the process begins, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nA(2)=5+7{e}^{2}-7=7{e}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nThus, after $2$ days, the process has produced $7{e}^{2}-2$ kilograms of compost." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318521 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319130 1069 "q-346580" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319130 1071 "In $2010$, a group of biologists started tracking the population of wolves in a mountain reserve. The initial population was $250,$ and the birth and death rates are given by $6+2t$ and $2+0.5t,$ respectively, where $t$ is the number of years since $2010.$  \n\nFind an expression for the population $P(t)$ of wolves in the reserve at any time $t>0.$\n\n$P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319130 1072 #uuid "48e677b7-771f-574c-af49-3e95d535ddf9" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319130 1074 "Let $p(t)$ be the rate of change of the population. According to the given information,\n\n$$\np(t)={{6+2t}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population increase}}-{{(2+0.5t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population decrease}}=4+1.5t.\n$$\n\nLet $P(T)$ represent the population of wolves $T$ years after $2010.$ By the accumulation of change formula, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(4+1.5t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =250+(4t+\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =250+4T+\\frac{3}{4}{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We get \n\n$$\nP(t)=250+\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}+4t.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318562 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319131 1069 "q-346582" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319131 1071 "An engineer started tracking the amount of electric charge stored in a battery. The initial charge was $75$ coulombs, and the charging and discharging rates are given by $6+{t}^{3/2}$ and $1+0.5t,$ respectively, where $t$ is the number of hours since the beginning of the observation.  \n\nFind an expression for the charge $C(t)$ stored in the battery at any time $t>0.$\n\n$C(t)=75-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319131 1072 #uuid "b623d6eb-0fce-5aac-ba26-711c0546ffa3" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319131 1074 "Let $c(t)$ be the rate of change of the charge. According to the given information,\n\n$$\nc(t)={{6+{t}^{3/2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{charge increase}}-{{(1+0.5t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{charge decrease}}=5+{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}t.\n$$\n\nLet $C(T)$ represent the charge stored $T$ hours after $2015.$ By the accumulation of change formula, we have\n\n$$\nC(T)=C(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}c(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(T) & =C(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}c(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =75+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(5+{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =75+(5t+\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =75+5T+\\frac{2}{5}{T}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{4}{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We get \n\n$$\nC(t)=75-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}+5t+\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318913 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319132 1069 "q-346606" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319132 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319132 1072 #uuid "03370258-b60b-5182-80c5-13bf5b04ada7" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319132 1074 "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-2}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/4a/4af4a2b7389d3773cc71e843190c6ab111355248a15e6165a8e39e37252061b9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318668 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319133 1069 "q-346615" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319133 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319133 1072 #uuid "609b4352-6bf8-54f6-ba10-486e4bcc6a92" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319133 1074 "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(5-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/83/83f7d663fa66184b56c4f79d61f6e17012714a3de7512202b3022e91d4325437.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318461 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319134 1069 "q-346618" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319134 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319134 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319134 1072 #uuid "695fa5d3-07df-5ef4-9c06-adb52aee0852" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319134 1074 "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{5}^{u}]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{a},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{5}^{a}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/c0/c0c7699b036ebd8779cd6c3d3143cdcca6312834ee731e144e38488124164f43.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318943 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319135 1069 "q-346621" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319135 1071 "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-5$ and $y=11.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319135 1072 #uuid "b3b0b076-b835-59ae-a7b6-3a733887db90" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319135 1074 "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](images/d2/d2398c3a8a04d005dd4d0c6e8b06046a6ca98aa86127204e7521341213dfb464.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5 & =11 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/ca/caf1c4d5df49aa18ed85158615be19745abb7c078269dde35f4e57d57fae292a.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=11.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(11)-({x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(16x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =[16\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}]-[16\\cdot (-4)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-4)^{3}] \\\\  & =(64-\\frac{64}{3})-(-64+\\frac{64}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318790 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319136 1069 "q-346643" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319136 1071 "Find the area $A$ of the closed region between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}-6$ and the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319136 1072 #uuid "f3b41f79-b110-546e-abb0-181ed99a24a2" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319136 1074 "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](images/c5/c5cd702bed84fd0ffc7f0e3a9510bdb3411bb8f77424786f9445c4d559392416.png)\n\nWe want the area between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}-6$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-4$ to $y=4.$\nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}-6)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-6{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}-6y\\,{|}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(64-(-64))-6(4-(-4)) \\\\  & =16-48 \\\\  & =-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So, the area that we want is $|-32|=32.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318922 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319137 1069 "q-346674" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319137 1071 "Find the area $A$ of the closed region between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y$ and the $y$-axis for $y\\le 0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319137 1072 #uuid "a2eb01ae-aca0-5076-9d1d-17516ca61cf1" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319137 1074 "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}y({y}^{4}-16) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}y({y}^{2}-4)({y}^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}y(y-2)(y+2)({y}^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](images/03/03737836a352eafaf486e7e5f71d882ac39715e788a0c9113eb809b185584bdc.png)\n\nWe want the area between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-2$ to $y=0.$\nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y-3{\\int }_{-2}^{0}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{y}^{6}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}(0-(-64))-\\frac{3}{2}(0-4) \\\\  & =-2+6 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318820 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319138 1069 "q-346702" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319138 1071 "![](images/78/7808295803d04d2c996ca7d36110f070a60f4491107ad1c831e6fa8ea9f85678.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319138 1072 #uuid "6f3ba61f-934e-53ed-a21e-8c527c008d63" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319138 1074 "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi y) & =1 \\\\ \\pi y & =…,-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},… \\\\ y & =…,-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4},…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that we want to find the area between the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-\\frac{3}{4}$ to $y=\\frac{1}{4}.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3/4}^{1/4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3/4}^{1/4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-3/4}^{1/4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\pi }|}_{-3/4}^{1/4}-{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\pi }|}_{-3/4}^{1/4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})]-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})]-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }|=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318773 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319139 1069 "q-346710" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319139 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319139 1072 #uuid "6f963953-4ca0-5b72-8632-5bdad8051d11" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319139 1074 "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-4}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-4)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx+2=A(x-4)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+2 & =A(4-4)+B \\\\ B & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+2 & =A(0-4)+6 \\\\ 2 & =-4A+6 \\\\ 4A & =4 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x-4}+\\frac{6}{(x-4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x-4}+\\frac{6}{(x-4)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x+6{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{6}{x-4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-4|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-4|)-(\\frac{6}{2-4}-\\frac{6}{1-4}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-(-3-(-2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318538 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318752 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319140 1069 "q-346718" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319140 1071 "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(2,1)$ and is concave down on the interval $(1,4).$ If ${f}^{′}(2)=1,$ estimate the value of $f(1.6)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.$\\,f(1.6)≈$ {{field-1}} and the approximation is an {{field-2}}." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319140 1072 #uuid "2bad2914-49c2-58b3-abd8-429c2410586f" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319140 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(2,1),$ so we have $f(2)=1.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(2)=1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =1+1(x-2) \\\\  & =x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.6) & ≈L(1.6) \\\\  & =1.6-1 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(1,4),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=2,$ and therefore we have an $\\text{overestimate}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318737 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319141 1069 "q-346735" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319141 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319141 1071 "Find the area $A$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x+\\frac{1}{4}}$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=-2$ and $y=-1.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319141 1072 #uuid "2de816ec-a60f-55ae-ac40-4e164bd6326f" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319141 1074 "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{x+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =x+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ x & ={y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}\\,|}_{-2}^{-1}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-4)-\\frac{1}{4}[-1-(-2)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So, the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-4|=4.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318825 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319142 1069 "q-346744" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319142 1071 "Find the area $A$ between the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319142 1072 #uuid "c31c75ff-7af4-5eb7-b07d-1625fcf88f0e" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319142 1074 "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) \\\\ {e}^{y} & =x+1 \\\\ x & ={e}^{y}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-y\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(e-1)-(1-0) \\\\  & =e-1-1 \\\\  & =e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318529 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319143 1069 "q-346752" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319143 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319143 1072 #uuid "68d214ab-9577-5e8b-9200-4e23d1e48429" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319143 1074 "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}=\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-1)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx-4=A(x-1)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-4 & =A(1-1)+B \\\\ B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-4 & =A(0-1)+B \\\\ -4 & =-A-3 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{3}{(x-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{3}{(x-1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|\\,{|}_{2}^{4}+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)+3(\\frac{1}{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{1})+3(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318643 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319144 1069 "q-346783" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319144 1071 "![](images/81/816b36f644f3e41d8f48bed2dd49e150d751cb13e082b6a1b9b4ad16973d3d57.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ between the curve $y=3\\sqrt{\\frac{x+3}{3}}$, the $y$-axis and the lines $y=1$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319144 1072 #uuid "b0202626-0cd9-5ad9-969e-84650f47d2b2" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319144 1074 "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{\\frac{x+3}{3}} \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{x+3}{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{x+3}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} & =x+3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-3)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-3{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,|}_{1}^{2}-3\\cdot y\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{3})-3(2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}-3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So, the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-\\frac{20}{9}|=\\frac{20}{9}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318568 13194139533419 17592186