17592186319285 1071 "Given that $r(t)=⟨cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}),\\,\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-4},\\,ln\\,(2\\,{e}^{t-2}-1)⟩,$ what is $|r(2)|?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$|r(2)|=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319285 1072 #uuid "467bbe03-18b4-513c-ba49-7eedf1cc8a92" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319285 1074 "Evaluating the function at $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}),\\,\\sqrt{{2}^{3}-4},\\,ln\\,(2\\,{e}^{0}-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ),\\,\\sqrt{4},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(2)| & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318829 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319286 1069 "q-348691" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319286 1071 "Given that $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t+3},\\,3\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩,$ what is $|r(1)|?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$|r(1)|=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319286 1072 #uuid "c027bb21-dcd4-5596-85c9-6da87679f319" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319286 1074 "Evaluating the function at $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\sqrt{1+3},\\,3\\,{e}^{1-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{4},\\,3\\,{e}^{0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(1)| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318467 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319287 1069 "q-348693" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319287 1071 "Given that $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,\\sqrt{t+3},\\,t-1⟩,$ what is $|r(1)|?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$|r(1)|=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319287 1072 #uuid "1bd1a588-edf0-5720-9e2d-306abd5f8af7" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319287 1074 "Evaluating the function at $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ),\\,\\sqrt{4},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(1)| & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318656 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319288 1069 "q-348705" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319288 1071 "![](images/ae/ae150f223d210225b6490bebb859909b07842a8b7abb41137cf0206312aead2e.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\pi ,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319288 1072 #uuid "3c835cb3-d34e-5a3e-93e9-faa531eb53cf" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319288 1074 "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318808 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319289 1069 "q-348715" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319289 1071 "![](images/41/41304b7f8cbaf3df778e0f079a1651d2846f94dc74012db3b2ce936144eac8c7.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319289 1072 #uuid "0693a2a7-f309-5977-8582-ff59fce11d62" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319289 1074 "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318542 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319290 1069 "q-348716" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319290 1071 "![](images/a2/a2ccde221d71e611f7917d6dac0d12ca6b8204d9cd80a21d79005195245cf77a.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{6}),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319290 1072 #uuid "5161378e-fb37-51cc-9749-945f16c22d40" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319290 1074 "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,{|}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318475 13194139533419 1