92186045585 true] [17592186319302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319302 1071 "![](images/77/77004c9040818fbc254b9564b9c5821536ef65fcc77f6765b56d2e8524c24ed4.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=6{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+1,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ and $x=0,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319302 1072 #uuid "3af0a07e-3893-5b2a-b9da-b80cdbb6630d" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319302 1074 "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-3\\pi /2}^{0}(6{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nRecall  ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+1 & =4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+2({sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3}))+1 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1.\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-3\\pi /2}^{0}(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{3})+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3\\pi /2}^{0}(2(cos\\,(\\frac{2x}{3})+1)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3\\pi /2}^{0}(2cos\\,(\\frac{2x}{3})+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(3sin\\,(\\frac{2x}{3})+5x)\\,|}_{-3\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+5\\cdot 0)-(3sin\\,(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))+5(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =(0+0)-(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )-\\frac{15\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =(0)-(0-\\frac{15\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318581 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319303 1069 "q-348766" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319303 1071 "![](images/5e/5e40f35729a8e3df9f8403cad85bf0c4e6045733616c86cc399548a901c03ac7.png)\n\nA region in the $xy$-plane is bounded by the curve $y=2{cos}^{2}⁡x+x-1,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. The area of the region is\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319303 1072 #uuid "1cdb7061-4680-5ba2-8dc7-9dd171022ade" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319303 1074 "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(2{cos}^{2}⁡x+x-1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate this integral, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(2{cos}^{2}⁡x+x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)+x-1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )+\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{2}]-[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318680 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319304 1069 "q-348837" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319304 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}({cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2})-2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}))\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319304 1072 #uuid "4f40df86-3936-5a06-ad55-95bff3a8fe95" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319304 1074 "Recall  ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ so letting $\\theta =\\frac{3x}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2})-2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}) & =1-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2})-2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}) \\\\  & =1-3{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{3x}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))={sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(1-3{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(1-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}x)\\,|}_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}]-[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}] \\\\  & =[0-\\frac{\\pi }{3}]-[0-\\frac{\\pi }{6}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318771 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319305 1069 "q-348844" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319305 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3\\pi }^{6\\pi }6{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319305 1072 #uuid "0e74c108-0ed1-5674-9470-01c9cf8b9752" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319305 1074 "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{6},$ we have\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{6}).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3\\pi }^{6\\pi }6{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{3\\pi }^{6\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{3\\pi }^{6\\pi }[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{[x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})]}_{3\\pi }^{6\\pi } \\\\  & =3[6\\pi -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})-(3\\pi -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{3}))] \\\\  & =3[6\\pi -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-(3\\pi -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))] \\\\  & =3(6\\pi -3\\pi ) \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318657 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319306 1069 "q-348845" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319306 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-5\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}(8{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319306 1072 #uuid "9c5e1c6b-1bd8-542e-a514-268b3f9603b7" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319306 1074 "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}(8{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-5\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}(4[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})]+4x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}(4-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})+4x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(4x-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})+2{x}^{2})|}_{-5\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[4\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{2}-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2(5\\pi /2)}{5})+2{(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})}^{2}] \\\\  & \\,-[4\\cdot (-\\frac{5\\pi }{2})-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2(-5\\pi /2)}{5})+2{(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2})}^{2}] \\\\  & =[10\\pi -10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2(\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{4})]-[-10\\pi -10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )+2(\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{4})] \\\\  & =[10\\pi -0+\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{2}]-[-10\\pi -0+\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =20\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318630 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319307 1069 "q-348849" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319307 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319307 1072 #uuid "c872c2cf-5759-524f-829f-1674c73a940a" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319307 1074 "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{8x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{8x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡x)(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{8})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{8})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{8}-2\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{8}-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{8}-2I \\\\ 3I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{8} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{24}({ln}^{3}⁡x)\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}({ln}^{3}⁡({e}^{3})-{ln}^{3}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}((3\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}({3}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318886 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319308 1069 "q-34885" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319308 1071 "Consider the curve$r(t)=(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\,i+(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,j,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by  $s=4t.$ Find $R(s),$ the parametrization of the curve by its arc length $s.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 tru