 denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x-8)(x+1).\n$$\n\nWe convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7x-11}{(x-8)(x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{x-8}+\\frac{B}{x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n7x-11=A(x+1)+B(x-8).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=8,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(8)-11 & =A(8+1)+B(8-8) \\\\ 45 & =9A \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(-1)-11 & =A(-1+1)+B(-1-8) \\\\ -18 & =-9B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7x-11}{(x-8)(x+1)}=\\frac{5}{x-8}+\\frac{2}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7x-11}{(x-8)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\frac{1}{x-8}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-8|+2\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318909 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319315 1069 "q-348917" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319315 1071 "![](images/07/07a1fab6073fdff2a20140719b570c0c40981ab049866480fbaff4dfad0c7799.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=\\frac{7}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2x),$ the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$, as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319315 1072 #uuid "b3aff197-7a38-5b3a-a2a7-53a6c2a8bdc0" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319315 1074 "The area of the region bounded by the curve $y=\\frac{7}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2x),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ is \n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{5\\pi /4}\\frac{7}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))={sin}^{2}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{5\\pi /4}\\frac{7}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{5\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{5\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{(x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))|}_{\\pi /4}^{5\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}[(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi ))-(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318716 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318718 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319316 1069 "q-348933" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319316 1071 "Evaluate the following indefinite integral and fill in the missing coefficients.\n\n$\\int \\frac{11}{(2x+5)(x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+5|\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+C$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319316 1072 #uuid "d78f678c-271e-5e6f-bc0e-d0b4c96c7b22" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319316 1074 "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x+5)(x-2).\n$$\n\nWe convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{(2x+5)(x-2)} & =\\frac{A}{2x+5}+\\frac{B}{x-2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n11=A(x-2)+B(2x+5).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =9B \\\\ B & =\\frac{11}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{5}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =-\\frac{9}{2}A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{22}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{(2x+5)(x-2)}=-\\frac{22}{9(2x+5)}+\\frac{11}{9(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11}{(2x+5)(x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{22}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{11}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{9}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+5|+\\frac{11}{9}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318865 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319317 1069 "q-348955" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319317 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{3}{13}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319317 1072 #uuid "2814a8ee-19f3-5bf7-ad86-ee8ee1974e91" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319317 1074 "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(3{e}^{3x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{3}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(3{e}^{3x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{3}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{3}{2}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{3}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{3}{2}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{9}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{13}{4}I & ={e}^{3x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{13}{e}^{3x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{1}{13}{e}^{3x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{13}{e}^{3(\\pi /2)}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )))-(\\frac{1}{13}{e}^{3(0)}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}{e}^{3\\pi /2}(2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-1))-\\frac{3}{13} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{13}({e}^{3\\pi /2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318741 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319318 1069 "q-348975" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319318 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319318 1072 #uuid "6b13617e-d037-5962-9e92-2f6869856914" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319318 1074 "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-8x+12=(x-2)(x-6).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12} & =\\frac{3x+7}{(x-2)(x-6)}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{x-6},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n3x+7=A(x-6)+B(x-2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(6)+7 & =A(6-6)+B(6-2) \\\\ 25 & =4B \\\\ B & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)+7 & =A(2-6)+B(2-2) \\\\ 13 & =-4A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{13}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12}=-\\frac{13}{4(x-2)}+\\frac{25}{4(x-6)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{13}{4(x-2)}+\\frac{25}{4(x-6)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1))-(-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318953 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319319 1069 "q-348976" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319319 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319319 1072 #uuid "87498ed9-b2f1-5890-a6bb-4cd539bb2116" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319319 1074 "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x-5=(x+5)(x-1).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{{