(Null}(A))=1.$\n- If $B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(B))=2.$\n- If $C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(C))=3.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319320 1072 #uuid "6b1d1e88-f02a-5243-9bdc-9d244b4db578" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319320 1074 "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The matrix $A$ has $1$ pivot column and $1$ non-pivot column. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The matrix $B$ has $1$ pivot column and $2$ non-pivot columns (namely, the $2$nd and $3$rd columns). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $C$ has $3$ pivot columns and no non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318732 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319321 1069 "q-349030" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319321 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319321 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{10}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319321 1072 #uuid "ef163074-a4b7-50f5-9864-4b40e28344ff" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319321 1074 "Let's denote the indefinite integral by  $I=\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-4x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(-4{e}^{-4x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-4x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})\\,(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(-4{e}^{-4x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation for $I,$ we solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-4I \\\\ 5I & =-{e}^{-4x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{10}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{10}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{10}(0+1))-(-\\frac{1}{10}(0+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(1-{e}^{-4\\pi }).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318595 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319322 1069 "q-349040" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319322 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319322 1072 #uuid "1545917f-fc9b-5e7b-a9c9-f1def306bc53" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319322 1074 "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n12{x}^{2}-x-6=(4x-3)(3x+2).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6} & =\\frac{36x-10}{(4x-3)(3x+2)}=\\frac{A}{4x-3}+\\frac{B}{3x+2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n36x-10=A(3x+2)+B(4x-3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\frac{3}{4},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(\\frac{3}{4})-10 & =A(3(\\frac{3}{4})+2)+B(4(\\frac{3}{4})-3) \\\\ 17 & =\\frac{17}{4}A \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(-\\frac{2}{3})-10 & =A(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2)+B(4(-\\frac{2}{3})-3) \\\\ -34 & =-\\frac{17}{3}B \\\\ B & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}=\\frac{4}{4x-3}+\\frac{6}{3x+2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{4x-3}+\\frac{6}{3x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x-3|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x-3|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(4x-3)(3x+2)^{2}|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(4x-3)(3x+2)^{2}|{|}_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{49}{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{49}{48}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{49}{48}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318449 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319323 1069 "q-349041" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319323 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319323 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319323 1072 #uuid "e0a37242-8ca3-5cb0-9c32-a06114fb7f40" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319323 1074 "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-19x-4=(5x+1)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4} & =\\frac{15x-39}{(5x+1)(x-4)}=\\frac{A}{5x+1}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n15x-39=A(x-4)+B(5x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(4)-39 & =A(4-4)+B(5(4)+1) \\\\ 21 & =21B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for 