me{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{4}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}(2\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{4}{9}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}(2\\pi \\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)+\\frac{4}{9}(0-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319339 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318557 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319339 1069 "q-349297" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319339 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319339 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}9x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319339 1072 #uuid "615dee59-7992-5c1c-8fe1-9c3970b38307" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319339 1074 "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=9x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=9 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 9x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =9x\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\cdot 9\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}9x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 0)+(-1-1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318574 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319340 1069 "q-349305" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319340 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319340 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(-18x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319340 1072 #uuid "dfc2da83-70ec-5b4d-8276-3941e4e2e19f" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319340 1074 "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-18x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=-18 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (-18x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(-18x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\cdot (-18)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(-18x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}+6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-6(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-6(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 0)-2(-1-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318854 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319341 1069 "q-349348" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319341 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}9x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319341 1072 #uuid "fce587bb-2230-5419-afb1-775f458fc405" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319341 1074 "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=9x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=9 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x} & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 9x\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =9x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x})\\cdot 9\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}9x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3x{e}^{-3x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3x{e}^{-3x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-{e}^{-3x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-3(1\\cdot {e}^{-3}-0\\cdot {e}^{0})-({e}^{-3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3}-({e}^{-3}-1) \\\\  & =1-4{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318694 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319342 1069 "q-349354" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319342 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319342 1071 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=9$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=-1+4(x-9)-13(x-9)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{″}(9)?$\n\n${f}^{″}(9)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319342 1072 #uuid "d4f67dee-5149-57df-a860-8acc208f99c6" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319342 1074 "This is an expansion about the point $x=9,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(9).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-13.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(9)=-13\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(9)=-26.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319343 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318787 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319343 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318921 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319343 1069 "q-349355" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319343 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319343 1071 "Find $F(t)$ given that \n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=⟨\\frac{4}{t+1}\\,,3\\sqrt{t}⟩\n$$\n\n and $F(0)=⟨2,\\,-1⟩.$\n\n$F(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319343 1072 #uuid "cb63ef91-6d79-5cc4-8e36-d05f26d326da" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319343 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨\\frac{4}{t+1}\\,,3\\sqrt{t}⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\frac{4}{t+1}\\,,3\\sqrt{t}⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int \\frac{4}{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 3\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+{c}_{1},\\,2{t}^{\\frac{3}{2}}+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $F(0)=⟨2,\\,-1⟩$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0)=⟨4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+{c}_{1},\\,2(0)^{\\frac{3}{2}}+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨2,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=-1.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+2,\\,2{t}^{3/2}-1⟩.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318468 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319344 1069 "q-349356" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319344 1071 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=-10$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=7-8(x+10)+15(x+10)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{″}(-10)?$\n\n${f}^{″}(-10)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319344 1072 #uuid "d15048a1-8d0c-59dd-89d7-780b0e4dd83c" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319344 1074 "This is an expansion about the point $x=-10,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-10).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $15.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-10)=15\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(-10)=30.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318912 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318939 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319345 1069 "q-349375" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319345 1071 "Find $F(t)$ given that \n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=⟨2{e}^{-t}\\,,6{t}^{2}-2⟩\n$$\n\n and $F(0)=⟨1,\\,-4⟩.$\n\n$F(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319345 1072 #uuid "4cfd056c-ccd5-5c10-8bc7-7694f33e96ed" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319345 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨2{e}^{-t}\\,,6{t}^{2}-2⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨2{e}^{-t}\\,,6{t}^{2}-2⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int 2{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 6{t}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{e}^{-t}+{c}_{1},\\,2{t}^{3}-2t+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $F(0)=⟨1,\\,-4⟩$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0)=⟨-2{e}^{0}+{c}_{1},\\,2(0)^{3}-2(0)+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨1,\\,-4⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2+{c}_{1},\\,0+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨1,\\,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=3$ and ${c}_{2}=-4.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=⟨-2{e}^{-t}+3,\\,2{t}^{3}-2t-4⟩.\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318699 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319346 1069 "q-349452" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319346 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=4\\sqrt{t},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{s}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319346 1072 #uuid "1460731e-e50b-5497-9d82-f51781f57c3d" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319346 1074 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4\\sqrt{t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{7}{3}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3} & =\\frac{8}{3}(1)^{3/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & =\\frac{8}{3}+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 8{t}^{3/2} & =1 \\\\ {t}^{3/2} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ t & ={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2/3}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318469 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318818 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319347 1069 "q-349469" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319347 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319347 1071 "Given that $r(t)=⟨{e}^{2t}(t+1),\\,\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}}⟩,$ what is ${r}^{′}(0)?$\n\n${r}^{′}(0)=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319347 1072 #uuid "7ac22d1b-7887-5459-acc4-59652caa97eb" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319347 1074 "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)={e}^{2t}(t+1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =(2{e}^{2t})(t+1)+{e}^{2t}(1) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(t+1)+{e}^{2t},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=2{e}^{2\\cdot 0}(0+1)+{e}^{2\\cdot 0}=3.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)=\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}},$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{9-{0}^{2}}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{x}^{′}(0),\\,{y}^{′}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318440 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319348 1069 "q-34947" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319348 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319348 1072 #uuid "ade7d6b0-58df-5513-bc22-de237a49cc42" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319348 1074 "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2k-8 \\\\ k+4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2k-8=0 \\\\ k+4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=-4.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318832 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318835 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319349 1069 "q-349470" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319349 1071 "Given that $r(t)=⟨4+3{t}^{2},\\,4{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-1⟩,$ what is ${r}^{′}(1)?$\n\n${r}^{′}(1)=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319349 1072 #uuid "c81ce7cd-b6de-57e1-b7dc-68eb6562402f" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319349 1074 "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=4+3{t}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6t,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(1)=6\\cdot 1=6.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)=4{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=12{t}^{2}+6t,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(1)=12\\cdot {1}^{2}+6\\cdot 1=18.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1) & =⟨{x}^{′}(1),\\,{y}^{′}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,18⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318869 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319350 1069 "q-349595" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319350 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}4{e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319350 1072 #uuid "d36c1c11-19b9-5940-860c-a11b298fedf7" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319350 1074 "If we let $u=5x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}4{e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5})}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5})}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}({2}^{5}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}(32-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot 31 \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318764 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319351 1069 "q-349596" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319351 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}5{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319351 1072 #uuid "b9d82eda-7286-547a-83ef-0ce6d0452b88" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319351 1074 "If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $3\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}5{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{3})}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{3})}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}({4}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}(64-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}(63) \\\\  & =105\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318746 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319352 1069 "q-349601" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319352 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}4{e}^{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319352 1072 #uuid "8852114f-8bc0-5518-9880-52e4b44b23ec" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319352 1074 "If we let $u=4x+1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{e}^{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{9}{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\,{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & ={e}^{9}-{e}^{1} \\\\  & ={e}^{9}-e\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318792 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319353 1069 "q-349625" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319353 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}{3}^{7-2x}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319353 1072 #uuid "94f9e06c-ebba-5efe-8b0d-89981a71a1e9" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319353 1074 "If we let $u=7-2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{3}^{7-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{5}^{3}{3}^{u}(-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{3}{3}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}){|}_{5}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{3}-{3}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(27-243) \\\\  & =\\frac{108}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318824 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319354 1069 "q-349626" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319354 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}3\\cdot {2}^{5-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319354 1072 #uuid "84ff8262-4ace-5df5-9e7e-b8458e5e65ea" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319354 1074 "If we let $u=5-x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,-\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}3\\cdot {2}^{5-x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{2}3\\cdot {2}^{u}(-1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{4}^{2}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3(\\frac{{2}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}){|}_{4}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{2}-{2}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(4-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318690 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319355 1069 "q-349631" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319355 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\cdot {4}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319355 1072 #uuid "efb81182-b47b-5e4a-b191-d85df24ba0ef" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319355 1074 "If we let $u=1-x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,-\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\cdot {4}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}2\\cdot {4}^{u}(-1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{1}^{0}{4}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{{4}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}){|}_{1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({4}^{0}-{4}^{1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(1-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318622 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319356 1069 "q-349646" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319356 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}{5}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319356 1072 #uuid "442a9420-19fe-5c7f-9765-ddb48edbec27" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319356 1074 "If we let $u=x-1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{5}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{5}^{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}({5}^{1}-{5}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(5-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318777 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319357 1069 "q-349648" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319357 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{3}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319357 1072 #uuid "5ff1abc0-7cbc-5bd4-915a-56c8cc58e75d" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319357 1074 "If we let $u=x+1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{3}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{3}^{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{2}-{3}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(9-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318591 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319358 1069 "q-34973" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319358 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.$\n- If $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(B))=3.$\n- If $C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(C))=2.$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319358 1072 #uuid "56ebc46d-299b-5fda-a402-a93bacc04806" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319358 1074 "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The matrix $A$ has $3$ pivot columns and $2$ non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The matrix $B$ has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $4$th column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $C$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $2$nd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318930 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319359 1069 "q-349752" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319359 1071 "A laboratory is tracking the rate at which a chemical is being produced in a reaction. The rate $r(t)$ at which the chemical is produced is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in milligrams per hour, and $0<t<5$ is the time in hours since $1\\,\\text{pm}.$\n\nBetween $t=1$ and $t=3$ hours,\n{{field-1}} milligrams were produced." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319359 1072 #uuid "b1fe170f-2220-5047-8a07-fed1325d6108" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319359 1074 "We're given the **rate of change** of chemical production $r(t).$ To find the **net amount** of chemical produced by the reaction between the times $t=1$ and $t=3,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t & ={(\\frac{2}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{3}{3}{t}^{3}+4t)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}-{t}^{3}+4t)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}(3)^{4}-(3)^{3}+4(3))-(\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{4}-(1)^{3}+4(1)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{81}{2}-27+12)-(\\frac{1}{2}-1+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=1$ and $t=3$ hours, $22$ milligrams were produced." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318796 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319360 1069 "q-349765" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319360 1071 "A concert venue is tracking the rate at which guests enter the building. The rate $r(t)$ at which guests arrive is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\sqrt{t+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes.\n\nBetween $t=0$ and $t=3$ minutes, to one decimal place,\n{{field-1}} guests arrived." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319360 1072 #uuid "6d1270fa-bbe7-59d3-a6b5-67a300ca1db7" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319360 1074 "We're given the **rate of change** in the number of guests, $r(t).$ To find the number of guests that arrived between the times $t=0$ and $t=3$ minutes, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}3\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =3\\,\\frac{2(t+1)^{3/2}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2(t+1)^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2(3+1)^{3/2}-2(0+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =2(4)^{3/2}-2(1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =16-2 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=0$ and $t=3$ minutes, $14$ guests arrived." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318895 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319361 1069 "q-349771" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319361 1071 "A website is tracking the rate at which new users sign up. The rate $r(t)$ at which users sign up is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=12\\cdot {2}^{t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in users per day, and $t>0$ is the time in days.\n\nBetween $t=3$ and $t=9$ days, to nearest integer,\n{{field-1}} users signed up." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319361 1072 #uuid "46ec9578-0045-566d-b42f-3167d4e3673f" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319361 1074 "We are given the signup rate $r(t)$ in users per day. To find the number of users that signed up between the times $t=3$ and $t=9$ days, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{9}12\\cdot {2}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={12\\cdot \\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{t/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & ={36\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{t/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{3}-{2}^{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{216}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈312\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, between $t=3$ and $t=9$ days, $312$ users signed up." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318713 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319362 1069 "q-3498" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319362 1071 "$\\frac{8n}{2n-6}÷\\frac{4n}{10n-30}=$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319362 1072 #uuid "92034418-3c29-508e-a258-197f92ef596d" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319362 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{8n}{2n-6}÷\\frac{4n}{10n-30},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8n}{2n-6}÷\\frac{4n}{10n-30} & = \\\\ \\frac{8n}{2n-6}\\cdot \\frac{10n-30}{4n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4n\\cdot 2}{2n-6}\\cdot \\frac{5(2n-6)}{4n} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4n}\\cdot 2}{\\cancel{2n-6}}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cancel{(2n-6)}}{\\cancel{4n}} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot 5 & = \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318797 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319363 1069 "q-349982" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319363 1071 "Engineers are testing a water pump filling a reservoir.\nThey observe that water enters the reservoir at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=12\\sqrt{t+4},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in gallons per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes since the pump was turned on.\nAt the same time, water leaks from the reservoir at a constant rate of $6$ gallons per minute.\n\nIf the reservoir was initially empty, find the expression that gives $V(t),$ the volume of water in the reservoir $t$ minutes after the pump was turned on.\n\n$V(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,6t-64$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319363 1072 #uuid "cf47ded2-1412-5eb7-b966-1f22d44b4bb0" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319363 1074 "Water is entering the reservoir at a rate of $r(t)$ gallons per minute and is leaking at a rate of $6$ gallons per minute. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the volume of water is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-6 \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{t+4}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total volume $V(t)$ after $t$ minutes, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(t) & =V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}(12\\sqrt{x+4}-6)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. Also, since the reservoir is initially empty, we set $V(0)=0.$\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}12\\sqrt{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={12\\cdot \\frac{2(x+4)^{3/2}}{3}|}_{0}^{t}-6x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =8(t+4)^{3/2}-8(4)^{3/2}-6t \\\\  & =8(t+4)^{3/2}-6t-64\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318480 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319364 1069 "q-349983" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319364 1071 "A zoo is tracking the number of penguins in a habitat.\nThey observe that new penguins arrive at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=30{e}^{-t/2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in penguins per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the tracking began.\nAt the same time, $3$ penguins are transferred out each month.\n\nIf there were initially $200$ penguins in the habitat, find the expression that gives $P(t),$ the number of penguins in the habitat $t$ months after the tracking began.\n\n$P(t)=-60{e}^{-t/2}\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319364 1072 #uuid "894497b2-7451-5370-bf78-0958e2308d18" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319364 1074 "The number of penguins is increasing at a rate of $r(t)$ penguins per month and is decreasing at a rate of $3$ penguins per month. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the number of penguins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-3 \\\\  & =30{e}^{-t/2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number $P(t)$ of penguins after $t$ months, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(30{e}^{-x/2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}30{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+(-60{e}^{-x/2}){|}_{0}^{t}-3x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =200+(60-60{e}^{-t/2})-3t \\\\  & =-60{e}^{-t/2}+260-3t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318516 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318621 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319365 1069 "q-349996" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319365 1071 "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319365 1072 #uuid "8b0963b7-9111-575b-994d-33c581e812aa" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319365 1074 "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n48-3{t}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=4,$ we figure out $N(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(4) & =60+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =60+{[48x-{x}^{3}]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =60+[(48\\cdot 4-{4}^{3})-(0)] \\\\  & =60+[192-64] \\\\  & =60+128 \\\\  & =188\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $60<188$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $188$, and this will happen after $4$ hours." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318476 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318639 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319366 1069 "q-349998" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319366 1071 "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=50+5{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319366 1072 #uuid "dbb58dca-50f3-5413-a2ce-ef8c814d6b01" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319366 1074 "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50+5{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(6-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n5(6-2t)=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=3,$ we figure out $N(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =50+5{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+5{[6x-{x}^{2}]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =50+5[(6\\cdot 3-{3}^{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =50+5\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $50<95$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $95$, and this will happen after $3$ hours." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318429 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318654 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319367 1069 "q-350001" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319367 1071 "The value $P(t)$ of a painting is given by the function\n\n$$\nP(t)=250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is measured in dollars, and $t>0$ is the time (in years) since the painting was created. \n\nAt what time is the value a minimum, and how much does the painting cost at this time, rounded to the nearest dollar?\n\nThe minimum value is\n$$$\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nyears." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319367 1072 #uuid "799a9b42-4f6e-5c6d-8236-7829c2135c09" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319367 1074 "To find when the value is minimized, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={3}^{t/4}-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{t/4}-27 & =0 \\\\ {3}^{t/4} & ={3}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{t}{4} & =3 \\\\ t & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=12.$ To find out the value of the painting when $t=12,$ we figure out $P(12):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{12}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =250+{[\\frac{4\\cdot {3}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-27x]|}_{0}^{12} \\\\  & =250+[(\\frac{4\\cdot 27}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-324)-(\\frac{4\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})] \\\\  & =250-324+\\frac{108-4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-74+\\frac{104}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & ≈21,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nSince $21<250,$ we conclude that the minimum value is $$\\,21$, and this happens after $12$ years." 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318629 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319368 1069 "q-350040" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319368 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319368 1072 #uuid "60a6f7c5-9743-5406-97a4-9c3469a873e7" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319368 1074 "If we let $u=2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318503 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319369 1069 "q-350060" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319369 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}3{sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319369 1072 #uuid "8ccae74f-35c6-57d0-a03f-750b899cc692" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319369 1074 "If we let $u=x-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\pi /4$ | $0$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}3{sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{0} \\\\  & =3(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318681 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319370 1069 "q-350071" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319370 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319370 1072 #uuid "d188146f-1019-54e0-bddf-9c317ac884d3" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319370 1074 "If we let $u=x-\\fra