\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $2x$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =1-\\frac{(2x)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2x)^{4}}{24}-⋯ \\\\  & =1-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{16{x}^{4}}{24}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the expansions for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =(4x-\\frac{32{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{256{x}^{5}}{30}-⋯)+(1-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{16{x}^{4}}{24}-⋯) \\\\  & =1+4x-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{32{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318448 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319372 1069 "q-350202" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319372 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /12}}8xsec\\,(4{x}^{2})tan\\,(4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319372 1072 #uuid "b2f52a91-e2df-5743-9b16-3551c628a81b" 13194139533419 17592186045585 true] [17592186319372 1074 "If we let $u=4{x}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=8x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{\\pi /12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /12}}8xsec\\,(4{x}^{2})tan\\,(4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /12}}sec\\,(4{x}^{2})tan\\,(4{x}^{2})\\cdot 8x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 131941395